---
title: Migrating to TanStack Table V9 (React)
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-s-new-in-tanstack-table-v9","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What's New in TanStack Table V9"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table V9 delivers major performance improvements, hundreds of bug fixes, new and refreshed features, and optional helpers for composing and managing tables. Despite the scale of the release, the headless model, core table logic, column definitions, and rendering patterns remain familiar. Here are the key changes:"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"1-better-performance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1. Better Performance"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lower memory usage"}]},{"type":"text","value":": The core architecture now shares more behavior across table objects, with some large-table scenarios seeing up to 90% memory savings."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Faster client-side row models"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Sorting, filtering, and aggregation paths have improved algorithms and memoization, with many scenarios seeing up to 40-70% speed improvements."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Better column resizing performance"}]},{"type":"text","value":": The same architectural and memoization work also speeds up column resizing."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"2-state-management-overhaul-backward-compatible","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2. State Management Overhaul (Backward Compatible)"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Compiler compatibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":": The state system is built on "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/store","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Store"}]},{"type":"text","value":", so the table now works correctly under the React Compiler. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./react-compiler","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Compiler Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fine-grained subscriptions"}]},{"type":"text","value":": State slices can be read independently through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state"},{"type":"text","value":", selectors, or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"External state or atoms"}]},{"type":"text","value":": You can still use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on[State]Change"},{"type":"text","value":", or own individual slices with writable atoms via the new "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" option."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"3-type-safety-improvements","children":[{"type":"text","value":"3. Type-Safety Improvements"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"New and revamped type helpers"}]},{"type":"text","value":": There are helpers for defining columns, custom filters, sorts, aggregations, column and table meta, shared table options and components, and more!"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Per-table meta types"}]},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableMeta"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnMeta"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterMeta"},{"type":"text","value":" slots let you type meta for a specific table instead of globally augmenting shared interfaces. "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No more global declaration merging required!"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature-gated APIs"}]},{"type":"text","value":": APIs only exist when their feature is registered, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures()"},{"type":"text","value":" validates feature prerequisites at the type level."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"4-tree-shaking-and-extensibility","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4. Tree Shaking and Extensibility"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import only the features you use"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Tables that only need sorting do not ship filtering, pagination, or other unused feature code. Start with 5kb of bundled JS and only bundle the features you need."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tree-shakeable row models and functions"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Row model factories and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFns"},{"type":"text","value":" now live on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures()"},{"type":"text","value":", so unused processing code can be dropped."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom features use the same system"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Your own feature plugins can register state, options, and APIs alongside the built-in features. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./custom-features.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Features Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"5-composability","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5. Composability"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableOptions"},{"type":"text","value":": Compose reusable table configuration, including features, row models, and default options."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":": Create custom table hooks with pre-bound features and components when you need a reusable app-level table pattern. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./composable-tables.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"composable-tables (createTableHook) guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"6-new-and-refreshed-features","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6. New and Refreshed Features"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"New Features"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cell Selection"}]},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cellSelectionFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" adds spreadsheet-style rectangular cell range selection, with drag, Shift-extend, and multiple disjoint ranges. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./cell-selection.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cell Selection Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cell Spanning"}]},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cellSpanningFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" merges body cells across rows and columns ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spanRows"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"spanColumns"},{"type":"text","value":", with span-aware cell selection), and header groups now compute "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header.rowSpan"},{"type":"text","value":" so shallow columns can span header rows. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./cell-spanning.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cell Spanning Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Refreshed Features"}]}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"More capable features"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Aggregation, Row Selection, Column Pinning, and Column Resizing have all been made more feature rich (multiple aggregation definitions per column, Shift range selection, logical "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"end"},{"type":"text","value":" pinning, and more)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"New core APIs"}]},{"type":"text","value":": New table and row APIs (like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getMaxSubRowDepth()"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.getDisplayIndex()"},{"type":"text","value":") round out the core feature set."}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"7-modern-builds","children":[{"type":"text","value":"7. Modern Builds"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ESM-only"}]},{"type":"text","value":": UMD and CJS builds have been dropped. Packages ship as modern ESM."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript target "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ES2022"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Compiled output now targets ES2022."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Smaller install size"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Published packages no longer ship "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"src"},{"type":"text","value":" or source maps, which reduces install footprint."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"the-good-news-most-upgrades-are-opt-in","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Good News: Most Upgrades Are Opt-in"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While Table V9 is a significant upgrade, "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"you don't have to adopt everything at once"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Don't want to optimize renders yet?"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Do nothing special. The default selector selects all registered state, so rendering works like Table V8."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Don't want to think about tree-shaking?"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stockFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" to include all features, just like Table V8."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table markup is largely unchanged."}]},{"type":"text","value":" How you render "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<table>"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<thead>"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<tr>"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<td>"},{"type":"text","value":", etc. remains the same."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The main change is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"how you define a table"}]},{"type":"text","value":" with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":" hook, specifically the new "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" option and where row model factories are registered."}]},{"type":"thematicBreak"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"quick-legacy-migration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quick Legacy Migration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Need to migrate incrementally? We are providing a temporary shortcut with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLegacyTable"},{"type":"text","value":" hook. It accepts the Table V8-style API while using Table V9 under the hood. "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is deprecated"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and intended only as a temporary migration aid. It includes all features by default, so your bundle will be even larger than it was with Table V8."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Legacy APIs live in a separate export. Import core utilities from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/react-table"},{"type":"text","value":" and legacy-specific APIs from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/react-table/legacy"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { flexRender } from '@tanstack/react-table'\nimport {\n  useLegacyTable,\n  getCoreRowModel,\n  getFilteredRowModel,\n  getSortedRowModel,\n  getPaginationRowModel,\n  legacyCreateColumnHelper,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table/legacy'","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./use-legacy-table.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"useLegacyTable Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for full documentation, examples, and type helpers."}]},{"type":"thematicBreak"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The rest of this guide focuses on migrating to the full Table V9 API and taking advantage of its features."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"core-breaking-changes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Core Breaking Changes"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"hook-rename","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hook Rename"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The number one change: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useReactTable"},{"type":"text","value":" is now "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":", consistent with hook naming across all TanStack libraries:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Table V8\nimport { useReactTable } from '@tanstack/react-table'\nconst table = useReactTable(options)\n\n// Table V9\nimport { useTable } from '@tanstack/react-table'\nconst table = useTable(options)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"new-required-features-table-option","children":[{"type":"text","value":"New Required "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" Table Option"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In Table V9, you must explicitly declare which features your table uses. Features, Row Models, and Row Model processing \"Fns\" are defined on the new "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" table option."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In Table V8, all features were bundled and included in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useReactTable"},{"type":"text","value":" hook. In Table V9, you import only what you need."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Table V8\nimport {\n  useReactTable,\n  getCoreRowModel,\n  getSortedRowModel,\n  sortingFns,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst table = useReactTable({\n  columns,\n  data,\n  getCoreRowModel: getCoreRowModel(),\n  getSortedRowModel: getSortedRowModel(),\n  sortingFns,\n})\n\n// Table V9\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  sortFns,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\n// All table options that concern including code modules (features, row models, Fns, etc.)\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // new - import and pass the feature you want to use\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // now row models are defined on the features object\n  sortFns, // now Fns are defined on the features object\n  // ...more features, row models, etc.\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features, // new required option\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"shortcut-use-stockfeatures-for-table-v8-like-behavior","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shortcut: Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stockFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" for Table V8-like Behavior"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want all features without having to think about it (like Table V8), import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stockFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useTable, stockFeatures } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features: stockFeatures, // All features included - just like Table V8 (though larger bundle now)\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"available-features","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Available Features"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import Name"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Faceting"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnFacetingFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Filtering"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnFilteringFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Grouping"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnGroupingFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Ordering"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrderingFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Pinning"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnPinningFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Resizing"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnResizingFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Sizing"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnSizingFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Visibility"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnVisibilityFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global Filtering"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"globalFilteringFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Aggregation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowAggregationFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Expanding"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowExpandingFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Pagination"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowPaginationFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Pinning"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowPinningFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Selection"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowSelectionFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Sorting"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowSortingFeature"}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"row-model-factories","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Model Factories"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row models are the functions that process your data (filtering, sorting, pagination, etc.). In Table V9, row model factories live on the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures({})"},{"type":"text","value":" call rather than a separate table option. The processing function registries ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFns"},{"type":"text","value":") are also registered on features. This enables better tree-shaking: you only bundle the row model code and filter/sort/aggregation functions you actually register."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row model slots are type-checked, so each row model must be specified after its associated feature in the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" call."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table V8 Option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table V9 "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" Slot"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table V9 Factory Function"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getCoreRowModel()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"(automatic)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not needed, always included"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getFilteredRowModel()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filteredRowModel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFilteredRowModel()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getSortedRowModel()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortedRowModel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSortedRowModel()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPaginationRowModel()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"paginatedRowModel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createPaginatedRowModel()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getExpandedRowModel()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expandedRowModel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createExpandedRowModel()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getGroupedRowModel()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupedRowModel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createGroupedRowModel()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getFacetedRowModel()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"facetedRowModel"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFacetedRowModel()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getFacetedMinMaxValues()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"facetedMinMaxValues"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFacetedMinMaxValues()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getFacetedUniqueValues()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"facetedUniqueValues"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFacetedUniqueValues()"}]}]]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  tableFeatures,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createGroupedRowModel,\n  filterFns, // Built-in filter functions\n  sortFns, // Built-in sort functions\n  aggregationFns, // Built-in aggregation functions\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowAggregationFeature,\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(),\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  filterFns,\n  sortFns,\n  aggregationFns,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"prefer-individual-fn-imports-over-full-registries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefer Individual Fn Imports Over Full Registries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFns"},{"type":"text","value":" registry exports are now deprecated in favor of importing individual "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFn_*"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn_*"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFn_*"},{"type":"text","value":" functions and registering only the ones you use (or passing functions directly in column definitions with no registration at all). The full registries still work, but spreading them puts every built-in function in your bundle. String names, including the default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'auto'"},{"type":"text","value":", only resolve functions you have registered."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Before: registers every built-in function\nimport { filterFns, sortFns } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  // ...other features and row models\n  filterFns,\n  sortFns,\n})\n\n// After: registers only the functions you use\nimport {\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  // ...other features and row models\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n  sortFns: { alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric, text: sortFn_text },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"instance-methods-must-be-called-on-their-instance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Instance Methods Must Be Called on Their Instance"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In Table V9, methods on rows, cells, columns, headers, and similar table objects are shared on the object's prototype instead of being created as arrow functions on each object. This improves memory usage, but it means destructuring those methods loses the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"this"},{"type":"text","value":" context they need to operate on the instance."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Table V8 - worked because getValue closed over the row object\nconst { getValue } = row\nconst value = getValue('name')\n\n// Table V9 - call the method on the instance\nconst value = row.getValue('name')","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This applies to row, cell, column, header, and related instance APIs, but not to the table instance itself. Audit code that destructures methods from table objects or passes them around as bare callbacks. Prefer calling them through the original object, for example "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.getValue('name')"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell.getContext()"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getCanSort()"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header.getContext()"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because these methods now live on the prototype, they also do not appear as own properties in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Object.keys(instance)"},{"type":"text","value":", object spread, or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"JSON.stringify"},{"type":"text","value":". A shallow clone like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ ...row }"},{"type":"text","value":" copies row data but does not copy row methods. The methods are still callable normally because JavaScript looks them up through the prototype chain."}]},{"type":"thematicBreak"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"state-management-changes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State Management Changes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table V9's state system is built on "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/store","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Store"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and exposes three read surfaces on the table instance:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Surface"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to use"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TSelected"},{"type":"text","value":" (full registered table state by default, or the shape returned from your custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":" selector)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The most ergonomic read surface inside a component rendered by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReadonlyStore<TableState>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A flat, framework-agnostic store of the entire table state. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store.state"},{"type":"text","value":" for one-off reads, or pair with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useSelector"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" for fine-grained subscriptions."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.<slice>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ReadonlyAtom<TableState[slice]>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A per-slice readonly atom. Subscribe to a single slice (e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.sorting"},{"type":"text","value":") when you want the narrowest possible re-render surface."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Writable counterparts (mostly internal):"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Surface"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to use"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.baseAtoms.<slice>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Atom<TableState[slice]>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The library's internal write target. You generally don't touch these directly; use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setSorting(...)"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setPagination(...)"},{"type":"text","value":", etc."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"options.atoms"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Partial<{ [slice]: Atom }>"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass in your own writable atom for any slice to take ownership of that state externally. See "},{"type":"link","href":"#external-atoms-advanced","children":[{"type":"text","value":"External Atoms"}]},{"type":"text","value":" below."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"accessing-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessing State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In Table V8, you accessed state via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getState()"},{"type":"text","value":". In Table V9, state is accessed differently:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Table V8\nconst state = table.getState()\nconst { sorting, pagination } = table.getState()\n\n// Table V9 (RECOMMENDED) - via table.state (full selected state by default)\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\nconst { sorting, pagination } = table.state\n\n// Table V9 - via the store (full state)\nconst fullState = table.store.state\nconst { sorting, pagination } = table.store.state\n\n// Table V9 - via table.state with a custom selector\nconst selectedTable = useTable(options, (state) => ({\n  sorting: state.sorting,\n  pagination: state.pagination,\n}))\n// Now selectedTable.state only contains sorting and pagination\nconst { sorting, pagination } = selectedTable.state\n\n// Table V9 - via a single slice atom (framework-agnostic, ideal for fine-grained subscriptions)\nconst sorting = table.atoms.sorting.get()","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"onstatechange-table-option-removed","children":[{"type":"text","value":"onStateChange Table Option Removed"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onStateChange"},{"type":"text","value":" table option was removed in Table V9, although individual "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on[State]Change"},{"type":"text","value":" handlers are still available for specific state slices."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want to lift or listen to any state change, you can set up a subscription to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const unsubscribe = table.store.subscribe((state) => {\n  console.log(state)\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"optimized-rendering-with-table-subscribe","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optimized Rendering with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The biggest state management improvement is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":", which enables fine-grained reactivity:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function MyTable() {\n  const table = useTable(\n    {\n      features,\n      columns,\n      data,\n    },\n    (state) => ({}), // default is state => state - this opts out of all default re-renders in favor of manual subscriptions down below\n  )\n\n  return (\n    <table.Subscribe\n      selector={(state) => ({\n        sorting: state.sorting,\n        pagination: state.pagination,\n      })}\n    >\n      {({ sorting, pagination }) => (\n        // This only re-renders when sorting or pagination changes\n        <div>\n          <table>{/* ... */}</table>\n          <div>Page {pagination.pageIndex + 1}</div>\n        </div>\n      )}\n    </table.Subscribe>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"default-table-v8-style-full-state-subscription","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default: Table V8-Style Full State Subscription"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default selector already gives Table V8-style behavior where the component re-renders on any registered table state change:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n\n// table.state contains the full registered state\nconst { sorting, pagination, columnFilters } = table.state","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Passing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"(state) => state"},{"type":"text","value":" is equivalent to the default and is no longer necessary. Pass a custom selector when you want "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state"},{"type":"text","value":" to contain only specific slices, or pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"() => null"},{"type":"text","value":" and use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" lower in the tree when the parent should not re-render for table state changes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"controlled-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Table V8-style "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on[State]Change"},{"type":"text","value":" controlled state patterns still work and remain convenient for simple integrations. For new Table V9 code, prefer owning state slices with external atoms (see "},{"type":"link","href":"#external-atoms-advanced","children":[{"type":"text","value":"External Atoms"}]},{"type":"text","value":" below), which give you fine-grained subscriptions without mirroring state through React:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const [sorting, setSorting] = useState<SortingState>([])\nconst [pagination, setPagination] = useState<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0,\n  pageSize: 10,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    sorting,\n    pagination,\n  },\n  onSortingChange: setSorting,\n  onPaginationChange: setPagination,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"per-slice-atom-subscriptions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Per-Slice Atom Subscriptions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because each state slice is backed by its own atom, you can subscribe a component to a single slice without re-rendering on any other state change. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useSelector"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/react-store"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.<slice>"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useSelector } from '@tanstack/react-store'\n\nfunction PaginationFooter({ table }) {\n  // Re-renders only when pagination changes. Sorting, filtering, selection, etc. are all ignored.\n  const pagination = useSelector(table.atoms.pagination)\n\n  return <div>Page {pagination.pageIndex + 1}</div>\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the narrowest subscription surface available. Compared to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":", which selects from the full "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store.state"},{"type":"text","value":", reading a per-slice atom skips even constructing the full state snapshot on change."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to reach for"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"vs."}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Both give you fine-grained re-renders. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" is nicer when you want to project multiple slices into a single rendered block. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.<slice>"},{"type":"text","value":" is nicer when a component only cares about one slice, or when you're passing a subscription source to non-table code."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"external-atoms-advanced","children":[{"type":"text","value":"External Atoms (Advanced)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For advanced patterns (sharing a slice across tables, integrating with atom-based libraries, or wiring a slice up to persistence), Table V9 lets you "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"own individual state slices yourself"}]},{"type":"text","value":" by passing writable atoms via the new "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" option. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../examples/basic-external-atoms","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic External Atoms example"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useCreateAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/react-store'\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n  sortFns,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\nimport type { PaginationState, SortingState } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature, rowPaginationFeature })\n\nfunction MyTable({ data, columns }) {\n  // Create stable external atoms for the slices you want to own.\n  const sortingAtom = useCreateAtom<SortingState>([])\n  const paginationAtom = useCreateAtom<PaginationState>({\n    pageIndex: 0,\n    pageSize: 10,\n  })\n\n  // Subscribe to each atom independently for fine-grained reactivity.\n  const sorting = useSelector(sortingAtom)\n  const pagination = useSelector(paginationAtom)\n\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n    // Per-slice external atoms. The library writes directly to these,\n    // bypassing the internal baseAtoms for those slices.\n    atoms: {\n      sorting: sortingAtom,\n      pagination: paginationAtom,\n    },\n  })\n\n  // Table writes like table.setPageIndex(2) go straight to `paginationAtom`.\n  // Any other subscriber of `paginationAtom` will see the update too.\n  // ...\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"how-external-atoms-interact-with-state-and-on-change","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How External Atoms Interact with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on*Change"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you register an external atom for a slice:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reads"}]},{"type":"text","value":": The derived "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms[slice]"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store.state[slice]"},{"type":"text","value":" both read from your external atom."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Writes"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Library writes (e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setSorting(...)"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.toggleSorting()"},{"type":"text","value":") go directly to your external atom's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"set()"},{"type":"text","value":". You do "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" need a corresponding "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSortingChange"},{"type":"text","value":" handler; owning the atom is the subscription."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Precedence"}]},{"type":"text","value":": If you pass both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"options.atoms[key]"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"options.state[key]"},{"type":"text","value":", the atom wins. If you pass neither, Table V9 falls back to its internal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"baseAtoms[key]"},{"type":"text","value":" (Table V8-style self-managed state)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reset"}]},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" does "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" clear external atoms. You own them, so you decide when to reset. Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"myAtom.set(defaultValue)"},{"type":"text","value":" yourself if needed."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"when-to-choose-external-atoms-vs-controlled-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to Choose External Atoms vs. Controlled State"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pattern"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use when"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Internal state (no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":", no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Simplest path; the table manages everything."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on*Change"},{"type":"text","value":" (Table V8-style controlled state)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want your framework's idiomatic state (React "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useState"},{"type":"text","value":", signals, etc.) to own the slice."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want atom-based ergonomics (cross-component subscriptions, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useSelector"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useAtom"},{"type":"text","value":") without the overhead of mirroring between React state and the table."}]}]]},{"type":"thematicBreak"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"feature-by-feature-breaking-changes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature-by-Feature Breaking Changes"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting-related APIs have been renamed for consistency:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table V8"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table V9"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortingFn"},{"type":"text","value":" (column def option)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getSortingFn()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getSortFn()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getAutoSortingFn()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getAutoSortFn()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SortingFn"},{"type":"text","value":" type"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" type"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SortingFns"},{"type":"text","value":" interface"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SortFns"},{"type":"text","value":" interface"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortingFns"},{"type":"text","value":" (built-in functions)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Update your column definitions:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Table V8\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n    sortingFn: 'alphanumeric', // or custom function\n  },\n]\n\n// Table V9\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n    sortFn: 'alphanumeric', // or custom function\n  },\n]","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"column-pinning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Pinning"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"V9 changes column pinning to use logical "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"end"},{"type":"text","value":" terminology instead of the physical "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"left"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"right"},{"type":"text","value":" terminology used in V8. In LTR languages/layouts, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start"},{"type":"text","value":" usually corresponds to left and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"end"},{"type":"text","value":" to right; in RTL languages/layouts, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start"},{"type":"text","value":" usually corresponds to right and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"end"},{"type":"text","value":" to left. There are no deprecated aliases."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"V8"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"V9"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnPinning.left"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnPinning.start"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnPinning.right"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnPinning.end"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.pin('left')"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.pin('start')"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.pin('right')"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.pin('end')"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getIsPinned() === 'left'"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getIsPinned() === 'start'"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getIsPinned() === 'right'"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getIsPinned() === 'end'"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.getLeftVisibleCells()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.getStartVisibleCells()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.getRightVisibleCells()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.getEndVisibleCells()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getLeftHeaderGroups()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getStartHeaderGroups()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getRightHeaderGroups()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getEndHeaderGroups()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getLeftLeafColumns()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getStartLeafColumns()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getRightLeafColumns()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getEndLeafColumns()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getLeftVisibleLeafColumns()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getStartVisibleLeafColumns()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getRightVisibleLeafColumns()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getEndVisibleLeafColumns()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getLeftTotalSize()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getStartTotalSize()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getRightTotalSize()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getEndTotalSize()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getStart('left')"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getStart('start')"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getAfter('right')"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getAfter('end')"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getIndex('left')"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getIndex('start')"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getIndex('right')"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getIndex('end')"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This rename is about logical table regions, not automatic DOM direction handling. For sticky column pinning, prefer CSS logical properties like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"insetInlineStart"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"insetInlineEnd"},{"type":"text","value":". The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnResizeDirection"},{"type":"text","value":" table option is unchanged."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enablePinning"},{"type":"text","value":" option has also been split into separate options:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Table V8\nenablePinning: true\n\n// Table V9\nenableColumnPinning: true\nenableRowPinning: true","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"column-sizing-vs-column-resizing-split","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Sizing vs. Column Resizing Split"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In Table V8, column sizing and resizing were combined in a single feature. In Table V9, they've been split into separate features for better tree-shaking."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table V8"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table V9"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ColumnSizing"},{"type":"text","value":" (combined feature)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnSizingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnResizingFeature"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnSizingInfo"},{"type":"text","value":" state"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnResizing"},{"type":"text","value":" state"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setColumnSizingInfo()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setColumnResizing()"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onColumnSizingInfoChange"},{"type":"text","value":" option"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onColumnResizingChange"},{"type":"text","value":" option"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you only need column sizing (fixed widths) without interactive resizing, you can import just "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnSizingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":". If you need drag-to-resize functionality, import both:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature, // Only if you need interactive resizing\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"grouping-and-aggregation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping and Aggregation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aggregation is now its own feature, independent from column grouping. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stockFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" still includes both, so tables using it need no feature-registration change. If you declare features explicitly, add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowAggregationFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" whenever columns use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFn"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregatedCell"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getAggregationValue"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell.getIsAggregated"},{"type":"text","value":". Add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnGroupingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" only when you also group rows."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({\n  rowAggregationFeature,\n  columnGroupingFeature, // only for grouped rows\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(),\n  aggregationFns: { sum: aggregationFn_sum },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom aggregation callables have changed to context-based definitions:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Table V8/earlier V9 betas\nconst total = (columnId, leafRows, childRows) =>\n  leafRows.reduce((sum, row) => sum + row.getValue(columnId), 0)\n\n// Current V9\nconst total = constructAggregationFn({\n  aggregate: ({ rows, getValue }) =>\n    rows.reduce((sum, row) => sum + Number(getValue(row)), 0),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The old per-function choice between "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"childRows"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"leafRows"},{"type":"text","value":" is replaced by a single depth-selected "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context.rows"},{"type":"text","value":", controlled by the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxAggregationDepth"},{"type":"text","value":" column option. The default ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":") preserves V8's direct-child grouped aggregation; use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Infinity"},{"type":"text","value":" to aggregate terminal leaf rows."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getAggregationValue()"},{"type":"text","value":" now takes a single options object instead of positional arguments:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Table V8/earlier V9 betas\ncolumn.getAggregationValue(rows, maxDepth)\n\n// Current V9\ncolumn.getAggregationValue({ rows, maxDepth })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getAggregationFn()"},{"type":"text","value":" is now "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.getAggregationFns()"},{"type":"text","value":" because a column can run multiple definitions, and the old callable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AggregationFn"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CreatedAggregationFn"},{"type":"text","value":" types are replaced by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AggregationFnDef"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./grouping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and the "},{"type":"link","href":"./aggregation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aggregation Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for full documentation of the new capabilities."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"row-selection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Selection"}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"warning","title":"Warning","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Minor breaking change:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.getToggleSelectedHandler()"},{"type":"text","value":" now enables inclusive Shift range selection by default when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowSelectionFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" is enabled. Existing checkboxes or rows wired through this handler establish an anchor on an ordinary interaction and select or deselect the current display-order range on a Shift interaction. Direct "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.toggleSelected()"},{"type":"text","value":" calls are unchanged."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enableRowRangeSelection: false"},{"type":"text","value":" to preserve the previous non-range handler behavior. The handler must receive an event that exposes Shift directly or through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nativeEvent"},{"type":"text","value":"; see "},{"type":"link","href":"./row-selection.md#shift-range-selection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shift Range Selection"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The \"some rows selected\" checks were simplified to mean \"at least one row is selected\":"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"API"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table V8"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table V9"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getIsSomeRowsSelected()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" when some but not all rows are selected"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" when at least one row is selected"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getIsSomePageRowsSelected()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" when some but not all page rows are selected"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" when at least one page row is selected"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In Table V8 these returned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"false"},{"type":"text","value":" once every row was selected; in Table V9 they stay "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":". If you use them to drive an indeterminate \"select all\" checkbox, gate the indeterminate state on the matching all-selected check so it clears at full selection:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getIsSomeRowsSelected() && !getIsAllRowsSelected()"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"row-and-internal-api-changes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row and Internal API Changes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Some row APIs have changed from private to public:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table V8"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table V9"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row._getAllCellsByColumnId()"},{"type":"text","value":" (private)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.getAllCellsByColumnId()"},{"type":"text","value":" (public)"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All other internal APIs prefixed with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"_"},{"type":"text","value":" have been removed. If you were using any of these, use their public equivalents:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Removed: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table._getPinnedRows()"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Removed: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table._getFacetedRowModel()"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Removed: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table._getFacetedMinMaxValues()"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Removed: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table._getFacetedUniqueValues()"}]}]}]},{"type":"thematicBreak"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"column-helper-changes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Helper Changes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createColumnHelper"},{"type":"text","value":" function now requires a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" type parameter in addition to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TData"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Table V8\nimport { createColumnHelper } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<Person>()\n\n// Table V9\nimport {\n  createColumnHelper,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature })\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"new-columns-helper-method","children":[{"type":"text","value":"New "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns()"},{"type":"text","value":" Helper Method"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table V9 adds a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns()"},{"type":"text","value":" helper for better type inference when wrapping column arrays. In Table V8, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TValue"},{"type":"text","value":" wasn't always type-safe, especially with group columns, where nested column types could be lost or widened. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns()"},{"type":"text","value":" helper uses variadic tuple types to preserve each column's individual "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TValue"},{"type":"text","value":" type, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"info.getValue()"},{"type":"text","value":" and cell renderers stay correctly typed throughout nested structures:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\n// Wrap your columns array for better type inference\nconst columns = columnHelper.columns([\n  columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  }),\n  columnHelper.accessor('lastName', {\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => <span>Last Name</span>,\n    cell: (info) => <i>{info.getValue()}</i>,\n  }),\n  columnHelper.display({\n    id: 'actions',\n    header: 'Actions',\n    cell: (info) => <button>Edit</button>,\n  }),\n])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"thematicBreak"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"rendering-changes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rendering Changes"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"flexrender-function","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flexRender"},{"type":"text","value":" Function"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flexRender"},{"type":"text","value":" function still exists and works the same way:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { flexRender } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\n// Still works in Table V9\nflexRender(cell.column.columnDef.cell, cell.getContext())\nflexRender(header.column.columnDef.header, header.getContext())","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"new-flexrender-and-table-flexrender-component","children":[{"type":"text","value":"New "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FlexRender"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<table.FlexRender />"},{"type":"text","value":" Component"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table V9 adds a cleaner component-based approach attached to the table instance:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const table = useTable({ /* ... */ })\n\n// Instead of:\n{flexRender(header.column.columnDef.header, header.getContext())}\n\n// You can use:\n<table.FlexRender header={header} />\n<table.FlexRender cell={cell} />\n<table.FlexRender footer={footer} />\n// or\nimport { FlexRender } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n<FlexRender header={header} />\n<FlexRender cell={cell} />\n<FlexRender footer={footer} />","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This should be way more convenient than the old "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flexRender"},{"type":"text","value":" function!"}]},{"type":"thematicBreak"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-tableoptions-utility","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" Utility"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" helper provides type-safe composition of table options. It's useful for creating reusable partial configurations that can be spread into your table setup."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"basic-usage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic Usage"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  tableOptions,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature })\n\n// Create a reusable options object with features pre-configured\nconst baseOptions = tableOptions({\n  features,\n  debugTable: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development',\n})\n\n// Use in your table; columns, data, and other options can be added\nconst table = useTable({\n  ...baseOptions,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"composing-partial-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composing Partial Options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" lets you omit required fields (like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":") when creating partial configurations:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Row model factories and fns registries are registered on the features object\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n  filterFns,\n})\n\n// Partial options without data or columns\nconst featureOptions = tableOptions({\n  features,\n  getRowId: (row) => row.id,\n  manualPagination: true,\n  manualSorting: true,\n  manualFiltering: true,\n})\n\n// Another partial without features (inherits from spread)\nconst paginationDefaults = tableOptions({\n  initialState: {\n    pagination: { pageIndex: 0, pageSize: 25 },\n  },\n})\n\n// Combine them\nconst table = useTable({\n  ...featureOptions,\n  ...paginationDefaults,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"using-with-createtablehook","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" pairs well with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":" for building composable table factories:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst sharedOptions = tableOptions({ features })\n\nconst { useAppTable } = createTableHook(sharedOptions)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"thematicBreak"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"createtablehook-composable-table-patterns","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":": Composable Table Patterns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is an advanced, optional feature."}]},{"type":"text","value":" You don't need to use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":" is sufficient for most use cases. If you're familiar with "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/form","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Form"}]},{"type":"text","value":"'s "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFormHook"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":" works almost the same way: it creates a custom hook with pre-bound configuration that you can reuse across many tables."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For applications with multiple tables sharing the same configuration, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":" lets you define features (including row model factories), and reusable components once:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// hooks/table.ts\nimport {\n  createTableHook,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n  filterFns,\n  sortFns,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\n// Import your reusable components\nimport { PaginationControls, SortIndicator, TextCell } from './components'\n\n// Features and row model factories defined once\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  filterFns,\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nexport const {\n  useAppTable,\n  createAppColumnHelper,\n  useTableContext,\n  useCellContext,\n  useHeaderContext,\n} = createTableHook({\n  features,\n\n  // Default table options\n  debugTable: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development',\n\n  // Register reusable components\n  tableComponents: { PaginationControls },\n  cellComponents: { TextCell },\n  headerComponents: { SortIndicator },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"using-useapptable","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useAppTable"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// features/users.tsx\nimport { useAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } from './hooks/table'\n\nconst columnHelper = createAppColumnHelper<Person>()\n\nconst columns = columnHelper.columns([\n  columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: ({ cell }) => <cell.TextCell />, // Pre-bound component!\n  }),\n])\n\nfunction UsersTable({ data }: { data: Person[] }) {\n  const table = useAppTable({\n    columns,\n    data,\n    // features (including row model factories) already configured!\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <table.AppTable>\n      <table>\n        <thead>\n          {table.getHeaderGroups().map((headerGroup) => (\n            <tr key={headerGroup.id}>\n              {headerGroup.headers.map((h) => (\n                <table.AppHeader header={h} key={h.id}>\n                  {(header) => (\n                    <th>\n                      <header.FlexRender />\n                      <header.SortIndicator />\n                    </th>\n                  )}\n                </table.AppHeader>\n              ))}\n            </tr>\n          ))}\n        </thead>\n        <tbody>\n          {table.getRowModel().rows.map((row) => (\n            <tr key={row.id}>\n              {row.getAllCells().map((c) => (\n                <table.AppCell cell={c} key={c.id}>\n                  {(cell) => (\n                    <td>\n                      <cell.FlexRender />\n                    </td>\n                  )}\n                </table.AppCell>\n              ))}\n            </tr>\n          ))}\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n      <table.PaginationControls />\n    </table.AppTable>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"context-hooks-for-components","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Context Hooks for Components"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Components registered via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":" can access their context:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// components/SortIndicator.tsx\nimport { useHeaderContext } from './hooks/table'\n\nexport function SortIndicator() {\n  const header = useHeaderContext()\n  const sorted = header.column.getIsSorted()\n\n  if (!sorted) return null\n  return sorted === 'asc' ? ' 🔼' : ' 🔽'\n}\n\n// components/TextCell.tsx\nimport { useCellContext } from './hooks/table'\n\nexport function TextCell() {\n  const cell = useCellContext()\n  return <span>{cell.getValue() as string}</span>\n}\n\n// components/PaginationControls.tsx\nimport { useTableContext } from './hooks/table'\n\nexport function PaginationControls() {\n  const table = useTableContext()\n\n  return (\n    <table.Subscribe selector={(s) => s.pagination}>\n      {(pagination) => (\n        <div>\n          <button onClick={() => table.previousPage()}>Previous</button>\n          <span>Page {pagination.pageIndex + 1}</span>\n          <button onClick={() => table.nextPage()}>Next</button>\n        </div>\n      )}\n    </table.Subscribe>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"thematicBreak"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"typescript-changes-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript Changes Summary"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"type-generics","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Generics"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most types now require a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" parameter:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Table V8\ntype Column<TData>\ntype ColumnDef<TData>\ntype Table<TData>\ntype Row<TData>\ntype Cell<TData, TValue>\n\n// Table V9\ntype Column<TFeatures, TData, TValue>\ntype ColumnDef<TFeatures, TData, TValue>\ntype Table<TFeatures, TData>\ntype Row<TFeatures, TData>\ntype Cell<TFeatures, TData, TValue>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"using-typeof-features","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"typeof features"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The easiest way to get the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" type is with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"typeof"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n})\n\n// Use typeof to get the type\ntype MyFeatures = typeof features\n\nconst columns: ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>[] = [...]\n\nfunction Filter({ column }: { column: Column<typeof features, Person, unknown> }) {\n  // ...\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"using-stockfeatures","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StockFeatures"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stockFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":", use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StockFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" type:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { StockFeatures, ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst columns: ColumnDef<StockFeatures, Person>[] = [...]","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"tablemeta-columnmeta-typing-changes","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableMeta"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ColumnMeta"},{"type":"text","value":" Typing Changes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No more declaration merging required! (Although it still works if you want to keep using it)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global declaration merging to extend "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableMeta"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ColumnMeta"},{"type":"text","value":" works exactly like it did in Table V8. The only change you need to make is updating the generics shape: both interfaces now take "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" as the first type parameter."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Table V8\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-table' {\n  interface ColumnMeta<TData, TValue> {\n    customProperty: string\n  }\n}\n\n// Table V9 - TFeatures is now the first parameter\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-table' {\n  interface ColumnMeta<TFeatures, TData, TValue> {\n    customProperty: string\n  }\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"That's all that's required if you want to keep declaring meta types globally."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optionally, Table V9 also adds a new way to declare meta types "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"per-table"}]},{"type":"text","value":" without declaration merging. You can use type-only "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableMeta"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnMeta"},{"type":"text","value":" slots on the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" option, which only affect tables created with that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" object:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  columnMeta: metaHelper<{ customProperty: string }>(),\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the new "},{"type":"link","href":"../../../guide/table-and-column-meta","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table and Column Meta Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for full details on both approaches."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"filterfns-sortfns-aggregationfns-filtermeta-augmentation-replaced-by-registry-slots","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FilterFns"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SortFns"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AggregationFns"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FilterMeta"},{"type":"text","value":" Augmentation Replaced by Registry Slots"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In Table V8, making a custom function usable as a string reference (like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFn: 'fuzzy'"},{"type":"text","value":") required "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"declare module"},{"type":"text","value":" augmentation of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FilterFns"},{"type":"text","value":" interface, and typing filter meta required augmenting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FilterMeta"},{"type":"text","value":". In Table V9, registering the function in the matching registry slot does both jobs with no global augmentation:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Table V8\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-table' {\n  interface FilterFns {\n    fuzzy: FilterFn<unknown>\n  }\n  interface FilterMeta {\n    itemRank: RankingInfo\n  }\n}\n\n// Table V9 - register in the slot; the key becomes a valid string value\ninterface FuzzyFilterMeta {\n  itemRank?: RankingInfo\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { fuzzy: fuzzyFilter },\n  filterMeta: metaHelper<FuzzyFilterMeta>(),\n})\n\n// 'fuzzy' now typechecks in column defs for tables using these features\ncolumnHelper.accessor('name', { filterFn: 'fuzzy' })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same pattern applies to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns"},{"type":"text","value":" (for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" string values) and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFns"},{"type":"text","value":" (for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFn"},{"type":"text","value":" string values). See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./fuzzy-filtering.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fuzzy Filtering Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a complete example."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"rowdata-type-restriction","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RowData"},{"type":"text","value":" Type Restriction"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RowData"},{"type":"text","value":" type is now more restrictive:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Table V8 - very permissive\ntype RowData = unknown\n\n// Table V9 - must be a record or array\ntype RowData = Record<string, any> | Array<any>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This change improves type safety. If you were passing unusual data types, ensure your data conforms to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Record<string, any>"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Array<any>"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"thematicBreak"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"migration-checklist","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Migration Checklist"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Update import: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useReactTable"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Define "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"features"},{"type":"text","value":" using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures()"},{"type":"text","value":" (or use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stockFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Migrate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"get*RowModel()"},{"type":"text","value":" options to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" slots (e.g. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel()"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Register "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterFns"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFns"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aggregationFns"},{"type":"text","value":" registries as slots on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" (row model factories no longer take arguments)"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Replace destructured row/cell/column/header methods with calls on the instance (for example, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"row.getValue('name')"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rename "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortingFn"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortFn"},{"type":"text","value":" in column definitions"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Update column pinning to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"end"},{"type":"text","value":" terminology ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnPinning.start"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"column.pin('end')"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getStart*"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getEnd*"},{"type":"text","value":" APIs)"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Replace "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enablePinning"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enableColumnPinning"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enableRowPinning"},{"type":"text","value":" if used"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rename "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnSizingInfo"},{"type":"text","value":" state → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnResizing"},{"type":"text","value":" (and related options)"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Convert custom aggregation callables to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"constructAggregationFn({ aggregate, merge? })"},{"type":"text","value":" definitions"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Update state access: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getState()"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store.state"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.state"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Update TypeScript types to include "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" generic"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Update "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createColumnHelper<TData>()"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createColumnHelper<TFeatures, TData>()"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TableMeta"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ColumnMeta"},{"type":"text","value":" declaration merging, add the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" generic to your augmentations (optionally, switch to the per-table "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableMeta"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnMeta"},{"type":"text","value":" feature slots)"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Replace "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"declare module"},{"type":"text","value":" augmentation of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FilterFns"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SortFns"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AggregationFns"},{"type":"text","value":" with registry-slot registration, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FilterMeta"},{"type":"text","value":" augmentation with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"filterMeta"},{"type":"text","value":" slot"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"(Optional) Add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" for render optimizations"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"(Optional) Subscribe to individual slices via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.<slice>"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useSelector"},{"type":"text","value":" for the narrowest re-renders"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"(Optional) Pass writable atoms via the new "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" option to own specific state slices externally"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"(Optional) Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" for composable configurations"}]}],"checked":false},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"(Optional) Migrate to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":" for reusable table patterns"}]}],"checked":false}]},{"type":"thematicBreak"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check out these examples to see Table V9 patterns in action:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/basic-use-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic useTable"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Simple table with the new "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTable"},{"type":"text","value":" hook"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/basic-use-legacy-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic useLegacyTable"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Migration example using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useLegacyTable"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/basic-use-app-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic useAppTable"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/basic-external-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic External State"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Classic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"on*Change"},{"type":"text","value":" controlled state"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/basic-external-atoms","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic External Atoms"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Owning state slices with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useCreateAtom"},{"type":"text","value":" + the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" option"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/filters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Filters"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Filtering with the new API"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Sorting with the new API"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/composable-tables","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composable Tables"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Advanced "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":" patterns"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/kitchen-sink","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Kitchen Sink"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - An example combining all features"}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"what-s-new-in-tanstack-table-v9","text":"What's New in TanStack Table V9","level":2},{"id":"1-better-performance","text":"1. Better Performance","level":3},{"id":"2-state-management-overhaul-backward-compatible","text":"2. State Management Overhaul (Backward Compatible)","level":3},{"id":"3-type-safety-improvements","text":"3. Type-Safety Improvements","level":3},{"id":"4-tree-shaking-and-extensibility","text":"4. Tree Shaking and Extensibility","level":3},{"id":"5-composability","text":"5. Composability","level":3},{"id":"6-new-and-refreshed-features","text":"6. New and Refreshed Features","level":3},{"id":"7-modern-builds","text":"7. Modern Builds","level":3},{"id":"the-good-news-most-upgrades-are-opt-in","text":"The Good News: Most Upgrades Are Opt-in","level":3},{"id":"quick-legacy-migration","text":"Quick Legacy Migration","level":2},{"id":"core-breaking-changes","text":"Core Breaking Changes","level":2},{"id":"hook-rename","text":"Hook Rename","level":3},{"id":"new-required-features-table-option","text":"New Required features Table Option","level":3},{"id":"shortcut-use-stockfeatures-for-table-v8-like-behavior","text":"Shortcut: Use stockFeatures for Table V8-like Behavior","level":4},{"id":"available-features","text":"Available Features","level":4},{"id":"row-model-factories","text":"Row Model Factories","level":3},{"id":"prefer-individual-fn-imports-over-full-registries","text":"Prefer Individual Fn Imports Over Full Registries","level":4},{"id":"instance-methods-must-be-called-on-their-instance","text":"Instance Methods Must Be Called on Their Instance","level":3},{"id":"state-management-changes","text":"State Management Changes","level":2},{"id":"accessing-state","text":"Accessing State","level":3},{"id":"onstatechange-table-option-removed","text":"onStateChange Table Option Removed","level":4},{"id":"optimized-rendering-with-table-subscribe","text":"Optimized Rendering with table.Subscribe","level":3},{"id":"default-table-v8-style-full-state-subscription","text":"Default: Table V8-Style Full State Subscription","level":3},{"id":"controlled-state","text":"Controlled State","level":3},{"id":"per-slice-atom-subscriptions","text":"Per-Slice Atom Subscriptions","level":3},{"id":"external-atoms-advanced","text":"External Atoms (Advanced)","level":3},{"id":"how-external-atoms-interact-with-state-and-on-change","text":"How External Atoms Interact with state and on*Change","level":4},{"id":"when-to-choose-external-atoms-vs-controlled-state","text":"When to Choose External Atoms vs. Controlled State","level":4},{"id":"feature-by-feature-breaking-changes","text":"Feature-by-Feature Breaking Changes","level":2},{"id":"sorting","text":"Sorting","level":3},{"id":"column-pinning","text":"Column Pinning","level":3},{"id":"column-sizing-vs-column-resizing-split","text":"Column Sizing vs. Column Resizing Split","level":3},{"id":"grouping-and-aggregation","text":"Grouping and Aggregation","level":3},{"id":"row-selection","text":"Row Selection","level":3},{"id":"row-and-internal-api-changes","text":"Row and Internal API Changes","level":3},{"id":"column-helper-changes","text":"Column Helper Changes","level":2},{"id":"new-columns-helper-method","text":"New columns() Helper Method","level":3},{"id":"rendering-changes","text":"Rendering Changes","level":2},{"id":"flexrender-function","text":"flexRender Function","level":3},{"id":"new-flexrender-and-table-flexrender-component","text":"New FlexRender and <table.FlexRender /> Component","level":3},{"id":"the-tableoptions-utility","text":"The tableOptions() Utility","level":2},{"id":"basic-usage","text":"Basic Usage","level":3},{"id":"composing-partial-options","text":"Composing Partial Options","level":3},{"id":"using-with-createtablehook","text":"Using with createTableHook","level":3},{"id":"createtablehook-composable-table-patterns","text":"createTableHook: Composable Table Patterns","level":2},{"id":"using-useapptable","text":"Using useAppTable","level":3},{"id":"context-hooks-for-components","text":"Context Hooks for Components","level":3},{"id":"typescript-changes-summary","text":"TypeScript Changes Summary","level":2},{"id":"type-generics","text":"Type Generics","level":3},{"id":"using-typeof-features","text":"Using typeof features","level":3},{"id":"using-stockfeatures","text":"Using StockFeatures","level":3},{"id":"tablemeta-columnmeta-typing-changes","text":"TableMeta/ColumnMeta Typing Changes","level":3},{"id":"filterfns-sortfns-aggregationfns-filtermeta-augmentation-replaced-by-registry-slots","text":"FilterFns/SortFns/AggregationFns/FilterMeta Augmentation Replaced by Registry Slots","level":3},{"id":"rowdata-type-restriction","text":"RowData Type Restriction","level":3},{"id":"migration-checklist","text":"Migration Checklist","level":2},{"id":"examples","text":"Examples","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## What's New in TanStack Table V9\n\nTanStack Table V9 delivers major performance improvements, hundreds of bug fixes, new and refreshed features, and optional helpers for composing and managing tables. Despite the scale of the release, the headless model, core table logic, column definitions, and rendering patterns remain familiar. Here are the key changes:\n\n### 1. Better Performance\n\n- **Lower memory usage**: The core architecture now shares more behavior across table objects, with some large-table scenarios seeing up to 90% memory savings.\n- **Faster client-side row models**: Sorting, filtering, and aggregation paths have improved algorithms and memoization, with many scenarios seeing up to 40-70% speed improvements.\n- **Better column resizing performance**: The same architectural and memoization work also speeds up column resizing.\n\n### 2. State Management Overhaul (Backward Compatible)\n\n- **React Compiler compatibility**: The state system is built on [TanStack Store](https://tanstack.com/store), so the table now works correctly under the React Compiler. See the [React Compiler Guide](./react-compiler).\n- **Fine-grained subscriptions**: State slices can be read independently through `table.atoms`, `table.store`, `table.state`, selectors, or `table.Subscribe`.\n- **External state or atoms**: You can still use `state` plus `on[State]Change`, or own individual slices with writable atoms via the new `atoms` option.\n\n### 3. Type-Safety Improvements\n\n- **New and revamped type helpers**: There are helpers for defining columns, custom filters, sorts, aggregations, column and table meta, shared table options and components, and more!\n- **Per-table meta types**: `tableMeta`, `columnMeta`, and `filterMeta` slots let you type meta for a specific table instead of globally augmenting shared interfaces. **No more global declaration merging required!**\n- **Feature-gated APIs**: APIs only exist when their feature is registered, and `tableFeatures()` validates feature prerequisites at the type level.\n\n### 4. Tree Shaking and Extensibility\n\n- **Import only the features you use**: Tables that only need sorting do not ship filtering, pagination, or other unused feature code. Start with 5kb of bundled JS and only bundle the features you need.\n- **Tree-shakeable row models and functions**: Row model factories and `filterFns` / `sortFns` / `aggregationFns` now live on `tableFeatures()`, so unused processing code can be dropped.\n- **Custom features use the same system**: Your own feature plugins can register state, options, and APIs alongside the built-in features. See the [Custom Features Guide](./custom-features.md).\n\n### 5. Composability\n\n- `tableOptions`: Compose reusable table configuration, including features, row models, and default options.\n- `createTableHook`: Create custom table hooks with pre-bound features and components when you need a reusable app-level table pattern. See the [composable-tables (createTableHook) guide](./composable-tables.md).\n\n### 6. New and Refreshed Features\n\n- **New Features**\n  - **Cell Selection**: `cellSelectionFeature` adds spreadsheet-style rectangular cell range selection, with drag, Shift-extend, and multiple disjoint ranges. See the [Cell Selection Guide](./cell-selection.md).\n  - **Cell Spanning**: `cellSpanningFeature` merges body cells across rows and columns (`spanRows` / `spanColumns`, with span-aware cell selection), and header groups now compute `header.rowSpan` so shallow columns can span header rows. See the [Cell Spanning Guide](./cell-spanning.md).\n- **Refreshed Features**\n  - **More capable features**: Aggregation, Row Selection, Column Pinning, and Column Resizing have all been made more feature rich (multiple aggregation definitions per column, Shift range selection, logical `start`/`end` pinning, and more).\n  - **New core APIs**: New table and row APIs (like `table.getMaxSubRowDepth()`, `row.getDisplayIndex()`) round out the core feature set.\n\n### 7. Modern Builds\n\n- **ESM-only**: UMD and CJS builds have been dropped. Packages ship as modern ESM.\n- **TypeScript target `ES2022`**: Compiled output now targets ES2022.\n- **Smaller install size**: Published packages no longer ship `src` or source maps, which reduces install footprint.\n\n### The Good News: Most Upgrades Are Opt-in\n\nWhile Table V9 is a significant upgrade, **you don't have to adopt everything at once**:\n\n- **Don't want to optimize renders yet?** Do nothing special. The default selector selects all registered state, so rendering works like Table V8.\n- **Don't want to think about tree-shaking?** Import `stockFeatures` to include all features, just like Table V8.\n- **Table markup is largely unchanged.** How you render `<table>`, `<thead>`, `<tr>`, `<td>`, etc. remains the same.\n\nThe main change is **how you define a table** with the `useTable` hook, specifically the new `features` option and where row model factories are registered.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Legacy Migration\n\nNeed to migrate incrementally? We are providing a temporary shortcut with the `useLegacyTable` hook. It accepts the Table V8-style API while using Table V9 under the hood. **This is deprecated** and intended only as a temporary migration aid. It includes all features by default, so your bundle will be even larger than it was with Table V8.\n\nLegacy APIs live in a separate export. Import core utilities from `@tanstack/react-table` and legacy-specific APIs from `@tanstack/react-table/legacy`:\n\n```tsx\nimport { flexRender } from '@tanstack/react-table'\nimport {\n  useLegacyTable,\n  getCoreRowModel,\n  getFilteredRowModel,\n  getSortedRowModel,\n  getPaginationRowModel,\n  legacyCreateColumnHelper,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table/legacy'\n```\n\nSee the [useLegacyTable Guide](./use-legacy-table.md) for full documentation, examples, and type helpers.\n\n---\n\nThe rest of this guide focuses on migrating to the full Table V9 API and taking advantage of its features.\n\n## Core Breaking Changes\n\n### Hook Rename\n\nThe number one change: `useReactTable` is now `useTable`, consistent with hook naming across all TanStack libraries:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\nimport { useReactTable } from '@tanstack/react-table'\nconst table = useReactTable(options)\n\n// Table V9\nimport { useTable } from '@tanstack/react-table'\nconst table = useTable(options)\n```\n\n### New Required `features` Table Option\n\nIn Table V9, you must explicitly declare which features your table uses. Features, Row Models, and Row Model processing \"Fns\" are defined on the new `features` table option.\n\nIn Table V8, all features were bundled and included in the `useReactTable` hook. In Table V9, you import only what you need.\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\nimport {\n  useReactTable,\n  getCoreRowModel,\n  getSortedRowModel,\n  sortingFns,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst table = useReactTable({\n  columns,\n  data,\n  getCoreRowModel: getCoreRowModel(),\n  getSortedRowModel: getSortedRowModel(),\n  sortingFns,\n})\n\n// Table V9\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  sortFns,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\n// All table options that concern including code modules (features, row models, Fns, etc.)\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // new - import and pass the feature you want to use\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // now row models are defined on the features object\n  sortFns, // now Fns are defined on the features object\n  // ...more features, row models, etc.\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features, // new required option\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n#### Shortcut: Use `stockFeatures` for Table V8-like Behavior\n\nIf you want all features without having to think about it (like Table V8), import `stockFeatures`:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useTable, stockFeatures } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features: stockFeatures, // All features included - just like Table V8 (though larger bundle now)\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n#### Available Features\n\n| Feature           | Import Name               |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------- |\n| Column Faceting   | `columnFacetingFeature`   |\n| Column Filtering  | `columnFilteringFeature`  |\n| Column Grouping   | `columnGroupingFeature`   |\n| Column Ordering   | `columnOrderingFeature`   |\n| Column Pinning    | `columnPinningFeature`    |\n| Column Resizing   | `columnResizingFeature`   |\n| Column Sizing     | `columnSizingFeature`     |\n| Column Visibility | `columnVisibilityFeature` |\n| Global Filtering  | `globalFilteringFeature`  |\n| Row Aggregation   | `rowAggregationFeature`   |\n| Row Expanding     | `rowExpandingFeature`     |\n| Row Pagination    | `rowPaginationFeature`    |\n| Row Pinning       | `rowPinningFeature`       |\n| Row Selection     | `rowSelectionFeature`     |\n| Row Sorting       | `rowSortingFeature`       |\n\n### Row Model Factories\n\nRow models are the functions that process your data (filtering, sorting, pagination, etc.). In Table V9, row model factories live on the `tableFeatures({})` call rather than a separate table option. The processing function registries (`filterFns`, `sortFns`, `aggregationFns`) are also registered on features. This enables better tree-shaking: you only bundle the row model code and filter/sort/aggregation functions you actually register.\n\nRow model slots are type-checked, so each row model must be specified after its associated feature in the same `tableFeatures` call.\n\n| Table V8 Option            | Table V9 `tableFeatures` Slot | Table V9 Factory Function     |\n| -------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `getCoreRowModel()`        | (automatic)                   | Not needed, always included   |\n| `getFilteredRowModel()`    | `filteredRowModel`            | `createFilteredRowModel()`    |\n| `getSortedRowModel()`      | `sortedRowModel`              | `createSortedRowModel()`      |\n| `getPaginationRowModel()`  | `paginatedRowModel`           | `createPaginatedRowModel()`   |\n| `getExpandedRowModel()`    | `expandedRowModel`            | `createExpandedRowModel()`    |\n| `getGroupedRowModel()`     | `groupedRowModel`             | `createGroupedRowModel()`     |\n| `getFacetedRowModel()`     | `facetedRowModel`             | `createFacetedRowModel()`     |\n| `getFacetedMinMaxValues()` | `facetedMinMaxValues`         | `createFacetedMinMaxValues()` |\n| `getFacetedUniqueValues()` | `facetedUniqueValues`         | `createFacetedUniqueValues()` |\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  tableFeatures,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createGroupedRowModel,\n  filterFns, // Built-in filter functions\n  sortFns, // Built-in sort functions\n  aggregationFns, // Built-in aggregation functions\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowAggregationFeature,\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(),\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  filterFns,\n  sortFns,\n  aggregationFns,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n#### Prefer Individual Fn Imports Over Full Registries\n\nThe `filterFns`, `sortFns`, and `aggregationFns` registry exports are now deprecated in favor of importing individual `filterFn_*`, `sortFn_*`, and `aggregationFn_*` functions and registering only the ones you use (or passing functions directly in column definitions with no registration at all). The full registries still work, but spreading them puts every built-in function in your bundle. String names, including the default `'auto'`, only resolve functions you have registered.\n\n```tsx\n// Before: registers every built-in function\nimport { filterFns, sortFns } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  // ...other features and row models\n  filterFns,\n  sortFns,\n})\n\n// After: registers only the functions you use\nimport {\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  // ...other features and row models\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n  sortFns: { alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric, text: sortFn_text },\n})\n```\n\n### Instance Methods Must Be Called on Their Instance\n\nIn Table V9, methods on rows, cells, columns, headers, and similar table objects are shared on the object's prototype instead of being created as arrow functions on each object. This improves memory usage, but it means destructuring those methods loses the `this` context they need to operate on the instance.\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8 - worked because getValue closed over the row object\nconst { getValue } = row\nconst value = getValue('name')\n\n// Table V9 - call the method on the instance\nconst value = row.getValue('name')\n```\n\nThis applies to row, cell, column, header, and related instance APIs, but not to the table instance itself. Audit code that destructures methods from table objects or passes them around as bare callbacks. Prefer calling them through the original object, for example `row.getValue('name')`, `cell.getContext()`, `column.getCanSort()`, or `header.getContext()`.\n\nBecause these methods now live on the prototype, they also do not appear as own properties in `Object.keys(instance)`, object spread, or `JSON.stringify`. A shallow clone like `{ ...row }` copies row data but does not copy row methods. The methods are still callable normally because JavaScript looks them up through the prototype chain.\n\n---\n\n## State Management Changes\n\nTable V9's state system is built on [TanStack Store](https://tanstack.com/store) and exposes three read surfaces on the table instance:\n\n| Surface               | Type                                                                                                             | When to use                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `table.state`         | `TSelected` (full registered table state by default, or the shape returned from your custom `useTable` selector) | The most ergonomic read surface inside a component rendered by `useTable`.                                                                                                            |\n| `table.store`         | `ReadonlyStore<TableState>`                                                                                      | A flat, framework-agnostic store of the entire table state. Use `table.store.state` for one-off reads, or pair with `useSelector` / `table.Subscribe` for fine-grained subscriptions. |\n| `table.atoms.<slice>` | `ReadonlyAtom<TableState[slice]>`                                                                                | A per-slice readonly atom. Subscribe to a single slice (e.g. `table.atoms.sorting`) when you want the narrowest possible re-render surface.                                           |\n\nWritable counterparts (mostly internal):\n\n| Surface                   | Type                         | When to use                                                                                                                                    |\n| ------------------------- | ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `table.baseAtoms.<slice>` | `Atom<TableState[slice]>`    | The library's internal write target. You generally don't touch these directly; use `table.setSorting(...)`, `table.setPagination(...)`, etc.   |\n| `options.atoms`           | `Partial<{ [slice]: Atom }>` | Pass in your own writable atom for any slice to take ownership of that state externally. See [External Atoms](#external-atoms-advanced) below. |\n\n### Accessing State\n\nIn Table V8, you accessed state via `table.getState()`. In Table V9, state is accessed differently:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\nconst state = table.getState()\nconst { sorting, pagination } = table.getState()\n\n// Table V9 (RECOMMENDED) - via table.state (full selected state by default)\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\nconst { sorting, pagination } = table.state\n\n// Table V9 - via the store (full state)\nconst fullState = table.store.state\nconst { sorting, pagination } = table.store.state\n\n// Table V9 - via table.state with a custom selector\nconst selectedTable = useTable(options, (state) => ({\n  sorting: state.sorting,\n  pagination: state.pagination,\n}))\n// Now selectedTable.state only contains sorting and pagination\nconst { sorting, pagination } = selectedTable.state\n\n// Table V9 - via a single slice atom (framework-agnostic, ideal for fine-grained subscriptions)\nconst sorting = table.atoms.sorting.get()\n```\n\n#### onStateChange Table Option Removed\n\nThe `onStateChange` table option was removed in Table V9, although individual `on[State]Change` handlers are still available for specific state slices.\n\nIf you want to lift or listen to any state change, you can set up a subscription to the `table.store`:\n\n```tsx\nconst unsubscribe = table.store.subscribe((state) => {\n  console.log(state)\n})\n```\n\n### Optimized Rendering with `table.Subscribe`\n\nThe biggest state management improvement is `table.Subscribe`, which enables fine-grained reactivity:\n\n```tsx\nfunction MyTable() {\n  const table = useTable(\n    {\n      features,\n      columns,\n      data,\n    },\n    (state) => ({}), // default is state => state - this opts out of all default re-renders in favor of manual subscriptions down below\n  )\n\n  return (\n    <table.Subscribe\n      selector={(state) => ({\n        sorting: state.sorting,\n        pagination: state.pagination,\n      })}\n    >\n      {({ sorting, pagination }) => (\n        // This only re-renders when sorting or pagination changes\n        <div>\n          <table>{/* ... */}</table>\n          <div>Page {pagination.pageIndex + 1}</div>\n        </div>\n      )}\n    </table.Subscribe>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n### Default: Table V8-Style Full State Subscription\n\nThe default selector already gives Table V8-style behavior where the component re-renders on any registered table state change:\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n\n// table.state contains the full registered state\nconst { sorting, pagination, columnFilters } = table.state\n```\n\nPassing `(state) => state` is equivalent to the default and is no longer necessary. Pass a custom selector when you want `table.state` to contain only specific slices, or pass `() => null` and use `table.Subscribe` lower in the tree when the parent should not re-render for table state changes.\n\n### Controlled State\n\nThe Table V8-style `state` + `on[State]Change` controlled state patterns still work and remain convenient for simple integrations. For new Table V9 code, prefer owning state slices with external atoms (see [External Atoms](#external-atoms-advanced) below), which give you fine-grained subscriptions without mirroring state through React:\n\n```tsx\nconst [sorting, setSorting] = useState<SortingState>([])\nconst [pagination, setPagination] = useState<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0,\n  pageSize: 10,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    sorting,\n    pagination,\n  },\n  onSortingChange: setSorting,\n  onPaginationChange: setPagination,\n})\n```\n\n### Per-Slice Atom Subscriptions\n\nBecause each state slice is backed by its own atom, you can subscribe a component to a single slice without re-rendering on any other state change. Use `useSelector` from `@tanstack/react-store` with `table.atoms.<slice>`:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useSelector } from '@tanstack/react-store'\n\nfunction PaginationFooter({ table }) {\n  // Re-renders only when pagination changes. Sorting, filtering, selection, etc. are all ignored.\n  const pagination = useSelector(table.atoms.pagination)\n\n  return <div>Page {pagination.pageIndex + 1}</div>\n}\n```\n\nThis is the narrowest subscription surface available. Compared to `table.Subscribe`, which selects from the full `table.store.state`, reading a per-slice atom skips even constructing the full state snapshot on change.\n\n> **When to reach for** `table.atoms` **vs.** `table.Subscribe`**:** Both give you fine-grained re-renders. `table.Subscribe` is nicer when you want to project multiple slices into a single rendered block. `table.atoms.<slice>` is nicer when a component only cares about one slice, or when you're passing a subscription source to non-table code.\n\n### External Atoms (Advanced)\n\nFor advanced patterns (sharing a slice across tables, integrating with atom-based libraries, or wiring a slice up to persistence), Table V9 lets you **own individual state slices yourself** by passing writable atoms via the new `atoms` option. See the [Basic External Atoms example](../examples/basic-external-atoms).\n\n```tsx\nimport { useCreateAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/react-store'\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n  sortFns,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\nimport type { PaginationState, SortingState } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature, rowPaginationFeature })\n\nfunction MyTable({ data, columns }) {\n  // Create stable external atoms for the slices you want to own.\n  const sortingAtom = useCreateAtom<SortingState>([])\n  const paginationAtom = useCreateAtom<PaginationState>({\n    pageIndex: 0,\n    pageSize: 10,\n  })\n\n  // Subscribe to each atom independently for fine-grained reactivity.\n  const sorting = useSelector(sortingAtom)\n  const pagination = useSelector(paginationAtom)\n\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n    // Per-slice external atoms. The library writes directly to these,\n    // bypassing the internal baseAtoms for those slices.\n    atoms: {\n      sorting: sortingAtom,\n      pagination: paginationAtom,\n    },\n  })\n\n  // Table writes like table.setPageIndex(2) go straight to `paginationAtom`.\n  // Any other subscriber of `paginationAtom` will see the update too.\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\n#### How External Atoms Interact with `state` and `on*Change`\n\nWhen you register an external atom for a slice:\n\n- **Reads**: The derived `table.atoms[slice]` and `table.store.state[slice]` both read from your external atom.\n- **Writes**: Library writes (e.g. `table.setSorting(...)`, `column.toggleSorting()`) go directly to your external atom's `set()`. You do **not** need a corresponding `onSortingChange` handler; owning the atom is the subscription.\n- **Precedence**: If you pass both `options.atoms[key]` and `options.state[key]`, the atom wins. If you pass neither, Table V9 falls back to its internal `baseAtoms[key]` (Table V8-style self-managed state).\n- **Reset**: `table.reset()` does **not** clear external atoms. You own them, so you decide when to reset. Call `myAtom.set(defaultValue)` yourself if needed.\n\n#### When to Choose External Atoms vs. Controlled State\n\n| Pattern                                                 | Use when                                                                                                                                                      |\n| ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Internal state (no `state`, no `atoms`)                 | Simplest path; the table manages everything.                                                                                                                  |\n| `state` + `on*Change` (Table V8-style controlled state) | You want your framework's idiomatic state (React `useState`, signals, etc.) to own the slice.                                                                 |\n| `atoms` option                                          | You want atom-based ergonomics (cross-component subscriptions, `useSelector`, `useAtom`) without the overhead of mirroring between React state and the table. |\n\n---\n\n## Feature-by-Feature Breaking Changes\n\n### Sorting\n\nSorting-related APIs have been renamed for consistency:\n\n| Table V8                          | Table V9                 |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------------------ |\n| `sortingFn` (column def option)   | `sortFn`                 |\n| `column.getSortingFn()`           | `column.getSortFn()`     |\n| `column.getAutoSortingFn()`       | `column.getAutoSortFn()` |\n| `SortingFn` type                  | `SortFn` type            |\n| `SortingFns` interface            | `SortFns` interface      |\n| `sortingFns` (built-in functions) | `sortFns`                |\n\nUpdate your column definitions:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n    sortingFn: 'alphanumeric', // or custom function\n  },\n]\n\n// Table V9\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n    sortFn: 'alphanumeric', // or custom function\n  },\n]\n```\n\n### Column Pinning\n\nV9 changes column pinning to use logical `start`/`end` terminology instead of the physical `left`/`right` terminology used in V8. In LTR languages/layouts, `start` usually corresponds to left and `end` to right; in RTL languages/layouts, `start` usually corresponds to right and `end` to left. There are no deprecated aliases.\n\n| V8                                   | V9                                   |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------ |\n| `columnPinning.left`                 | `columnPinning.start`                |\n| `columnPinning.right`                | `columnPinning.end`                  |\n| `column.pin('left')`                 | `column.pin('start')`                |\n| `column.pin('right')`                | `column.pin('end')`                  |\n| `column.getIsPinned() === 'left'`    | `column.getIsPinned() === 'start'`   |\n| `column.getIsPinned() === 'right'`   | `column.getIsPinned() === 'end'`     |\n| `row.getLeftVisibleCells()`          | `row.getStartVisibleCells()`         |\n| `row.getRightVisibleCells()`         | `row.getEndVisibleCells()`           |\n| `table.getLeftHeaderGroups()`        | `table.getStartHeaderGroups()`       |\n| `table.getRightHeaderGroups()`       | `table.getEndHeaderGroups()`         |\n| `table.getLeftLeafColumns()`         | `table.getStartLeafColumns()`        |\n| `table.getRightLeafColumns()`        | `table.getEndLeafColumns()`          |\n| `table.getLeftVisibleLeafColumns()`  | `table.getStartVisibleLeafColumns()` |\n| `table.getRightVisibleLeafColumns()` | `table.getEndVisibleLeafColumns()`   |\n| `table.getLeftTotalSize()`           | `table.getStartTotalSize()`          |\n| `table.getRightTotalSize()`          | `table.getEndTotalSize()`            |\n| `column.getStart('left')`            | `column.getStart('start')`           |\n| `column.getAfter('right')`           | `column.getAfter('end')`             |\n| `column.getIndex('left')`            | `column.getIndex('start')`           |\n| `column.getIndex('right')`           | `column.getIndex('end')`             |\n\nThis rename is about logical table regions, not automatic DOM direction handling. For sticky column pinning, prefer CSS logical properties like `insetInlineStart` and `insetInlineEnd`. The `columnResizeDirection` table option is unchanged.\n\nThe `enablePinning` option has also been split into separate options:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\nenablePinning: true\n\n// Table V9\nenableColumnPinning: true\nenableRowPinning: true\n```\n\n### Column Sizing vs. Column Resizing Split\n\nIn Table V8, column sizing and resizing were combined in a single feature. In Table V9, they've been split into separate features for better tree-shaking.\n\n| Table V8                          | Table V9                                        |\n| --------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `ColumnSizing` (combined feature) | `columnSizingFeature` + `columnResizingFeature` |\n| `columnSizingInfo` state          | `columnResizing` state                          |\n| `setColumnSizingInfo()`           | `setColumnResizing()`                           |\n| `onColumnSizingInfoChange` option | `onColumnResizingChange` option                 |\n\nIf you only need column sizing (fixed widths) without interactive resizing, you can import just `columnSizingFeature`. If you need drag-to-resize functionality, import both:\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature, // Only if you need interactive resizing\n})\n```\n\n### Grouping and Aggregation\n\nAggregation is now its own feature, independent from column grouping. `stockFeatures` still includes both, so tables using it need no feature-registration change. If you declare features explicitly, add `rowAggregationFeature` whenever columns use `aggregationFn`, `aggregatedCell`, `getAggregationValue`, or `cell.getIsAggregated`. Add `columnGroupingFeature` and `groupedRowModel` only when you also group rows.\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowAggregationFeature,\n  columnGroupingFeature, // only for grouped rows\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(),\n  aggregationFns: { sum: aggregationFn_sum },\n})\n```\n\nCustom aggregation callables have changed to context-based definitions:\n\n```ts\n// Table V8/earlier V9 betas\nconst total = (columnId, leafRows, childRows) =>\n  leafRows.reduce((sum, row) => sum + row.getValue(columnId), 0)\n\n// Current V9\nconst total = constructAggregationFn({\n  aggregate: ({ rows, getValue }) =>\n    rows.reduce((sum, row) => sum + Number(getValue(row)), 0),\n})\n```\n\nThe old per-function choice between `childRows` and `leafRows` is replaced by a single depth-selected `context.rows`, controlled by the `maxAggregationDepth` column option. The default (`0`) preserves V8's direct-child grouped aggregation; use `Infinity` to aggregate terminal leaf rows.\n\n`column.getAggregationValue()` now takes a single options object instead of positional arguments:\n\n```ts\n// Table V8/earlier V9 betas\ncolumn.getAggregationValue(rows, maxDepth)\n\n// Current V9\ncolumn.getAggregationValue({ rows, maxDepth })\n```\n\n`column.getAggregationFn()` is now `column.getAggregationFns()` because a column can run multiple definitions, and the old callable `AggregationFn`/`CreatedAggregationFn` types are replaced by `AggregationFnDef`.\n\nSee the [Grouping Guide](./grouping) and the [Aggregation Guide](./aggregation) for full documentation of the new capabilities.\n\n### Row Selection\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Minor breaking change:** `row.getToggleSelectedHandler()` now enables inclusive Shift range selection by default when `rowSelectionFeature` is enabled. Existing checkboxes or rows wired through this handler establish an anchor on an ordinary interaction and select or deselect the current display-order range on a Shift interaction. Direct `row.toggleSelected()` calls are unchanged.\n>\n> Set `enableRowRangeSelection: false` to preserve the previous non-range handler behavior. The handler must receive an event that exposes Shift directly or through `nativeEvent`; see [Shift Range Selection](./row-selection.md#shift-range-selection).\n\nThe \"some rows selected\" checks were simplified to mean \"at least one row is selected\":\n\n| API                                 | Table V8                                            | Table V9                                      |\n| ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `table.getIsSomeRowsSelected()`     | `true` when some but not all rows are selected      | `true` when at least one row is selected      |\n| `table.getIsSomePageRowsSelected()` | `true` when some but not all page rows are selected | `true` when at least one page row is selected |\n\nIn Table V8 these returned `false` once every row was selected; in Table V9 they stay `true`. If you use them to drive an indeterminate \"select all\" checkbox, gate the indeterminate state on the matching all-selected check so it clears at full selection:\n\n`getIsSomeRowsSelected() && !getIsAllRowsSelected()`\n\n### Row and Internal API Changes\n\nSome row APIs have changed from private to public:\n\n| Table V8                                 | Table V9                               |\n| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| `row._getAllCellsByColumnId()` (private) | `row.getAllCellsByColumnId()` (public) |\n\nAll other internal APIs prefixed with `_` have been removed. If you were using any of these, use their public equivalents:\n\n- Removed: `table._getPinnedRows()`\n- Removed: `table._getFacetedRowModel()`\n- Removed: `table._getFacetedMinMaxValues()`\n- Removed: `table._getFacetedUniqueValues()`\n\n---\n\n## Column Helper Changes\n\nThe `createColumnHelper` function now requires a `TFeatures` type parameter in addition to `TData`:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\nimport { createColumnHelper } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<Person>()\n\n// Table V9\nimport {\n  createColumnHelper,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature })\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n```\n\n### New `columns()` Helper Method\n\nTable V9 adds a `columns()` helper for better type inference when wrapping column arrays. In Table V8, `TValue` wasn't always type-safe, especially with group columns, where nested column types could be lost or widened. The `columns()` helper uses variadic tuple types to preserve each column's individual `TValue` type, so `info.getValue()` and cell renderers stay correctly typed throughout nested structures:\n\n```tsx\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\n// Wrap your columns array for better type inference\nconst columns = columnHelper.columns([\n  columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  }),\n  columnHelper.accessor('lastName', {\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => <span>Last Name</span>,\n    cell: (info) => <i>{info.getValue()}</i>,\n  }),\n  columnHelper.display({\n    id: 'actions',\n    header: 'Actions',\n    cell: (info) => <button>Edit</button>,\n  }),\n])\n```\n\n---\n\n## Rendering Changes\n\n### `flexRender` Function\n\nThe `flexRender` function still exists and works the same way:\n\n```tsx\nimport { flexRender } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\n// Still works in Table V9\nflexRender(cell.column.columnDef.cell, cell.getContext())\nflexRender(header.column.columnDef.header, header.getContext())\n```\n\n### New `FlexRender` and `<table.FlexRender />` Component\n\nTable V9 adds a cleaner component-based approach attached to the table instance:\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable({ /* ... */ })\n\n// Instead of:\n{flexRender(header.column.columnDef.header, header.getContext())}\n\n// You can use:\n<table.FlexRender header={header} />\n<table.FlexRender cell={cell} />\n<table.FlexRender footer={footer} />\n// or\nimport { FlexRender } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n<FlexRender header={header} />\n<FlexRender cell={cell} />\n<FlexRender footer={footer} />\n```\n\nThis should be way more convenient than the old `flexRender` function!\n\n---\n\n## The `tableOptions()` Utility\n\nThe `tableOptions()` helper provides type-safe composition of table options. It's useful for creating reusable partial configurations that can be spread into your table setup.\n\n### Basic Usage\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  tableOptions,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature })\n\n// Create a reusable options object with features pre-configured\nconst baseOptions = tableOptions({\n  features,\n  debugTable: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development',\n})\n\n// Use in your table; columns, data, and other options can be added\nconst table = useTable({\n  ...baseOptions,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n### Composing Partial Options\n\n`tableOptions()` lets you omit required fields (like `data`, `columns`, or `features`) when creating partial configurations:\n\n```tsx\n// Row model factories and fns registries are registered on the features object\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n  filterFns,\n})\n\n// Partial options without data or columns\nconst featureOptions = tableOptions({\n  features,\n  getRowId: (row) => row.id,\n  manualPagination: true,\n  manualSorting: true,\n  manualFiltering: true,\n})\n\n// Another partial without features (inherits from spread)\nconst paginationDefaults = tableOptions({\n  initialState: {\n    pagination: { pageIndex: 0, pageSize: 25 },\n  },\n})\n\n// Combine them\nconst table = useTable({\n  ...featureOptions,\n  ...paginationDefaults,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n### Using with `createTableHook`\n\n`tableOptions()` pairs well with `createTableHook` for building composable table factories:\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst sharedOptions = tableOptions({ features })\n\nconst { useAppTable } = createTableHook(sharedOptions)\n```\n\n---\n\n## `createTableHook`: Composable Table Patterns\n\n**This is an advanced, optional feature.** You don't need to use `createTableHook`; `useTable` is sufficient for most use cases. If you're familiar with [TanStack Form](https://tanstack.com/form)'s `createFormHook`, `createTableHook` works almost the same way: it creates a custom hook with pre-bound configuration that you can reuse across many tables.\n\nFor applications with multiple tables sharing the same configuration, `createTableHook` lets you define features (including row model factories), and reusable components once:\n\n```tsx\n// hooks/table.ts\nimport {\n  createTableHook,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n  filterFns,\n  sortFns,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\n// Import your reusable components\nimport { PaginationControls, SortIndicator, TextCell } from './components'\n\n// Features and row model factories defined once\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  filterFns,\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nexport const {\n  useAppTable,\n  createAppColumnHelper,\n  useTableContext,\n  useCellContext,\n  useHeaderContext,\n} = createTableHook({\n  features,\n\n  // Default table options\n  debugTable: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development',\n\n  // Register reusable components\n  tableComponents: { PaginationControls },\n  cellComponents: { TextCell },\n  headerComponents: { SortIndicator },\n})\n```\n\n### Using `useAppTable`\n\n```tsx\n// features/users.tsx\nimport { useAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } from './hooks/table'\n\nconst columnHelper = createAppColumnHelper<Person>()\n\nconst columns = columnHelper.columns([\n  columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: ({ cell }) => <cell.TextCell />, // Pre-bound component!\n  }),\n])\n\nfunction UsersTable({ data }: { data: Person[] }) {\n  const table = useAppTable({\n    columns,\n    data,\n    // features (including row model factories) already configured!\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <table.AppTable>\n      <table>\n        <thead>\n          {table.getHeaderGroups().map((headerGroup) => (\n            <tr key={headerGroup.id}>\n              {headerGroup.headers.map((h) => (\n                <table.AppHeader header={h} key={h.id}>\n                  {(header) => (\n                    <th>\n                      <header.FlexRender />\n                      <header.SortIndicator />\n                    </th>\n                  )}\n                </table.AppHeader>\n              ))}\n            </tr>\n          ))}\n        </thead>\n        <tbody>\n          {table.getRowModel().rows.map((row) => (\n            <tr key={row.id}>\n              {row.getAllCells().map((c) => (\n                <table.AppCell cell={c} key={c.id}>\n                  {(cell) => (\n                    <td>\n                      <cell.FlexRender />\n                    </td>\n                  )}\n                </table.AppCell>\n              ))}\n            </tr>\n          ))}\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n      <table.PaginationControls />\n    </table.AppTable>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n### Context Hooks for Components\n\nComponents registered via `createTableHook` can access their context:\n\n```tsx\n// components/SortIndicator.tsx\nimport { useHeaderContext } from './hooks/table'\n\nexport function SortIndicator() {\n  const header = useHeaderContext()\n  const sorted = header.column.getIsSorted()\n\n  if (!sorted) return null\n  return sorted === 'asc' ? ' 🔼' : ' 🔽'\n}\n\n// components/TextCell.tsx\nimport { useCellContext } from './hooks/table'\n\nexport function TextCell() {\n  const cell = useCellContext()\n  return <span>{cell.getValue() as string}</span>\n}\n\n// components/PaginationControls.tsx\nimport { useTableContext } from './hooks/table'\n\nexport function PaginationControls() {\n  const table = useTableContext()\n\n  return (\n    <table.Subscribe selector={(s) => s.pagination}>\n      {(pagination) => (\n        <div>\n          <button onClick={() => table.previousPage()}>Previous</button>\n          <span>Page {pagination.pageIndex + 1}</span>\n          <button onClick={() => table.nextPage()}>Next</button>\n        </div>\n      )}\n    </table.Subscribe>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## TypeScript Changes Summary\n\n### Type Generics\n\nMost types now require a `TFeatures` parameter:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\ntype Column<TData>\ntype ColumnDef<TData>\ntype Table<TData>\ntype Row<TData>\ntype Cell<TData, TValue>\n\n// Table V9\ntype Column<TFeatures, TData, TValue>\ntype ColumnDef<TFeatures, TData, TValue>\ntype Table<TFeatures, TData>\ntype Row<TFeatures, TData>\ntype Cell<TFeatures, TData, TValue>\n```\n\n### Using `typeof features`\n\nThe easiest way to get the `TFeatures` type is with `typeof`:\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n})\n\n// Use typeof to get the type\ntype MyFeatures = typeof features\n\nconst columns: ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>[] = [...]\n\nfunction Filter({ column }: { column: Column<typeof features, Person, unknown> }) {\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\n### Using `StockFeatures`\n\nIf using `stockFeatures` with `useTable`, use the `StockFeatures` type:\n\n```tsx\nimport type { StockFeatures, ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst columns: ColumnDef<StockFeatures, Person>[] = [...]\n```\n\n### `TableMeta`/`ColumnMeta` Typing Changes\n\nNo more declaration merging required! (Although it still works if you want to keep using it)\n\nGlobal declaration merging to extend `TableMeta` or `ColumnMeta` works exactly like it did in Table V8. The only change you need to make is updating the generics shape: both interfaces now take `TFeatures` as the first type parameter.\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-table' {\n  interface ColumnMeta<TData, TValue> {\n    customProperty: string\n  }\n}\n\n// Table V9 - TFeatures is now the first parameter\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-table' {\n  interface ColumnMeta<TFeatures, TData, TValue> {\n    customProperty: string\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThat's all that's required if you want to keep declaring meta types globally.\n\nOptionally, Table V9 also adds a new way to declare meta types **per-table** without declaration merging. You can use type-only `tableMeta`/`columnMeta` slots on the `features` option, which only affect tables created with that `features` object:\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  columnMeta: metaHelper<{ customProperty: string }>(),\n})\n```\n\nSee the new [Table and Column Meta Guide](../../../guide/table-and-column-meta) for full details on both approaches.\n\n### `FilterFns`/`SortFns`/`AggregationFns`/`FilterMeta` Augmentation Replaced by Registry Slots\n\nIn Table V8, making a custom function usable as a string reference (like `filterFn: 'fuzzy'`) required `declare module` augmentation of the `FilterFns` interface, and typing filter meta required augmenting `FilterMeta`. In Table V9, registering the function in the matching registry slot does both jobs with no global augmentation:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-table' {\n  interface FilterFns {\n    fuzzy: FilterFn<unknown>\n  }\n  interface FilterMeta {\n    itemRank: RankingInfo\n  }\n}\n\n// Table V9 - register in the slot; the key becomes a valid string value\ninterface FuzzyFilterMeta {\n  itemRank?: RankingInfo\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { fuzzy: fuzzyFilter },\n  filterMeta: metaHelper<FuzzyFilterMeta>(),\n})\n\n// 'fuzzy' now typechecks in column defs for tables using these features\ncolumnHelper.accessor('name', { filterFn: 'fuzzy' })\n```\n\nThe same pattern applies to `sortFns` (for `sortFn` string values) and `aggregationFns` (for `aggregationFn` string values). See the [Fuzzy Filtering Guide](./fuzzy-filtering.md) for a complete example.\n\n### `RowData` Type Restriction\n\nThe `RowData` type is now more restrictive:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8 - very permissive\ntype RowData = unknown\n\n// Table V9 - must be a record or array\ntype RowData = Record<string, any> | Array<any>\n```\n\nThis change improves type safety. If you were passing unusual data types, ensure your data conforms to `Record<string, any>` or `Array<any>`.\n\n---\n\n## Migration Checklist\n\n- [ ] Update import: `useReactTable` → `useTable`\n- [ ] Define `features` using `tableFeatures()` (or use `stockFeatures`)\n- [ ] Migrate `get*RowModel()` options to `tableFeatures` slots (e.g. `filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel()`)\n- [ ] Register `filterFns` / `sortFns` / `aggregationFns` registries as slots on `tableFeatures` (row model factories no longer take arguments)\n- [ ] Replace destructured row/cell/column/header methods with calls on the instance (for example, `row.getValue('name')`)\n- [ ] Rename `sortingFn` → `sortFn` in column definitions\n- [ ] Update column pinning to `start`/`end` terminology (`columnPinning.start`, `column.pin('end')`, `getStart*`/`getEnd*` APIs)\n- [ ] Replace `enablePinning` with `enableColumnPinning`/`enableRowPinning` if used\n- [ ] Rename `columnSizingInfo` state → `columnResizing` (and related options)\n- [ ] Convert custom aggregation callables to `constructAggregationFn({ aggregate, merge? })` definitions\n- [ ] Update state access: `table.getState()` → `table.store.state` or `table.state`\n- [ ] Update TypeScript types to include `TFeatures` generic\n- [ ] Update `createColumnHelper<TData>()` → `createColumnHelper<TFeatures, TData>()`\n- [ ] If you use `TableMeta`/`ColumnMeta` declaration merging, add the `TFeatures` generic to your augmentations (optionally, switch to the per-table `tableMeta`/`columnMeta` feature slots)\n- [ ] Replace `declare module` augmentation of `FilterFns`/`SortFns`/`AggregationFns` with registry-slot registration, and `FilterMeta` augmentation with the `filterMeta` slot\n- [ ] (Optional) Add `table.Subscribe` for render optimizations\n- [ ] (Optional) Subscribe to individual slices via `table.atoms.<slice>` + `useSelector` for the narrowest re-renders\n- [ ] (Optional) Pass writable atoms via the new `atoms` option to own specific state slices externally\n- [ ] (Optional) Use `tableOptions()` for composable configurations\n- [ ] (Optional) Migrate to `createTableHook` for reusable table patterns\n\n---\n\n## Examples\n\nCheck out these examples to see Table V9 patterns in action:\n\n- [Basic useTable](../examples/basic-use-table) - Simple table with the new `useTable` hook\n- [Basic useLegacyTable](../examples/basic-use-legacy-table) - Migration example using `useLegacyTable`\n- [Basic useAppTable](../examples/basic-use-app-table) - Using `createTableHook`\n- [Basic External State](../examples/basic-external-state) - Classic `state` + `on*Change` controlled state\n- [Basic External Atoms](../examples/basic-external-atoms) - Owning state slices with `useCreateAtom` + the `atoms` option\n- [Filters](../examples/filters) - Filtering with the new API\n- [Sorting](../examples/sorting) - Sorting with the new API\n- [Composable Tables](../examples/composable-tables) - Advanced `createTableHook` patterns\n- [Kitchen Sink](../examples/kitchen-sink) - An example combining all features\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Migrating to TanStack Table V9 (React)\n---\n\n## What's New in TanStack Table V9\n\nTanStack Table V9 delivers major performance improvements, hundreds of bug fixes, new and refreshed features, and optional helpers for composing and managing tables. Despite the scale of the release, the headless model, core table logic, column definitions, and rendering patterns remain familiar. Here are the key changes:\n\n### 1. Better Performance\n\n- **Lower memory usage**: The core architecture now shares more behavior across table objects, with some large-table scenarios seeing up to 90% memory savings.\n- **Faster client-side row models**: Sorting, filtering, and aggregation paths have improved algorithms and memoization, with many scenarios seeing up to 40-70% speed improvements.\n- **Better column resizing performance**: The same architectural and memoization work also speeds up column resizing.\n\n### 2. State Management Overhaul (Backward Compatible)\n\n- **React Compiler compatibility**: The state system is built on [TanStack Store](https://tanstack.com/store), so the table now works correctly under the React Compiler. See the [React Compiler Guide](./react-compiler).\n- **Fine-grained subscriptions**: State slices can be read independently through `table.atoms`, `table.store`, `table.state`, selectors, or `table.Subscribe`.\n- **External state or atoms**: You can still use `state` plus `on[State]Change`, or own individual slices with writable atoms via the new `atoms` option.\n\n### 3. Type-Safety Improvements\n\n- **New and revamped type helpers**: There are helpers for defining columns, custom filters, sorts, aggregations, column and table meta, shared table options and components, and more!\n- **Per-table meta types**: `tableMeta`, `columnMeta`, and `filterMeta` slots let you type meta for a specific table instead of globally augmenting shared interfaces. **No more global declaration merging required!**\n- **Feature-gated APIs**: APIs only exist when their feature is registered, and `tableFeatures()` validates feature prerequisites at the type level.\n\n### 4. Tree Shaking and Extensibility\n\n- **Import only the features you use**: Tables that only need sorting do not ship filtering, pagination, or other unused feature code. Start with 5kb of bundled JS and only bundle the features you need.\n- **Tree-shakeable row models and functions**: Row model factories and `filterFns` / `sortFns` / `aggregationFns` now live on `tableFeatures()`, so unused processing code can be dropped.\n- **Custom features use the same system**: Your own feature plugins can register state, options, and APIs alongside the built-in features. See the [Custom Features Guide](./custom-features.md).\n\n### 5. Composability\n\n- `tableOptions`: Compose reusable table configuration, including features, row models, and default options.\n- `createTableHook`: Create custom table hooks with pre-bound features and components when you need a reusable app-level table pattern. See the [composable-tables (createTableHook) guide](./composable-tables.md).\n\n### 6. New and Refreshed Features\n\n- **New Features**\n  - **Cell Selection**: `cellSelectionFeature` adds spreadsheet-style rectangular cell range selection, with drag, Shift-extend, and multiple disjoint ranges. See the [Cell Selection Guide](./cell-selection.md).\n  - **Cell Spanning**: `cellSpanningFeature` merges body cells across rows and columns (`spanRows` / `spanColumns`, with span-aware cell selection), and header groups now compute `header.rowSpan` so shallow columns can span header rows. See the [Cell Spanning Guide](./cell-spanning.md).\n- **Refreshed Features**\n  - **More capable features**: Aggregation, Row Selection, Column Pinning, and Column Resizing have all been made more feature rich (multiple aggregation definitions per column, Shift range selection, logical `start`/`end` pinning, and more).\n  - **New core APIs**: New table and row APIs (like `table.getMaxSubRowDepth()`, `row.getDisplayIndex()`) round out the core feature set.\n\n### 7. Modern Builds\n\n- **ESM-only**: UMD and CJS builds have been dropped. Packages ship as modern ESM.\n- **TypeScript target `ES2022`**: Compiled output now targets ES2022.\n- **Smaller install size**: Published packages no longer ship `src` or source maps, which reduces install footprint.\n\n### The Good News: Most Upgrades Are Opt-in\n\nWhile Table V9 is a significant upgrade, **you don't have to adopt everything at once**:\n\n- **Don't want to optimize renders yet?** Do nothing special. The default selector selects all registered state, so rendering works like Table V8.\n- **Don't want to think about tree-shaking?** Import `stockFeatures` to include all features, just like Table V8.\n- **Table markup is largely unchanged.** How you render `<table>`, `<thead>`, `<tr>`, `<td>`, etc. remains the same.\n\nThe main change is **how you define a table** with the `useTable` hook, specifically the new `features` option and where row model factories are registered.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Legacy Migration\n\nNeed to migrate incrementally? We are providing a temporary shortcut with the `useLegacyTable` hook. It accepts the Table V8-style API while using Table V9 under the hood. **This is deprecated** and intended only as a temporary migration aid. It includes all features by default, so your bundle will be even larger than it was with Table V8.\n\nLegacy APIs live in a separate export. Import core utilities from `@tanstack/react-table` and legacy-specific APIs from `@tanstack/react-table/legacy`:\n\n```tsx\nimport { flexRender } from '@tanstack/react-table'\nimport {\n  useLegacyTable,\n  getCoreRowModel,\n  getFilteredRowModel,\n  getSortedRowModel,\n  getPaginationRowModel,\n  legacyCreateColumnHelper,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table/legacy'\n```\n\nSee the [useLegacyTable Guide](./use-legacy-table.md) for full documentation, examples, and type helpers.\n\n---\n\nThe rest of this guide focuses on migrating to the full Table V9 API and taking advantage of its features.\n\n## Core Breaking Changes\n\n### Hook Rename\n\nThe number one change: `useReactTable` is now `useTable`, consistent with hook naming across all TanStack libraries:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\nimport { useReactTable } from '@tanstack/react-table'\nconst table = useReactTable(options)\n\n// Table V9\nimport { useTable } from '@tanstack/react-table'\nconst table = useTable(options)\n```\n\n### New Required `features` Table Option\n\nIn Table V9, you must explicitly declare which features your table uses. Features, Row Models, and Row Model processing \"Fns\" are defined on the new `features` table option.\n\nIn Table V8, all features were bundled and included in the `useReactTable` hook. In Table V9, you import only what you need.\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\nimport {\n  useReactTable,\n  getCoreRowModel,\n  getSortedRowModel,\n  sortingFns,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst table = useReactTable({\n  columns,\n  data,\n  getCoreRowModel: getCoreRowModel(),\n  getSortedRowModel: getSortedRowModel(),\n  sortingFns,\n})\n\n// Table V9\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  sortFns,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\n// All table options that concern including code modules (features, row models, Fns, etc.)\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // new - import and pass the feature you want to use\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // now row models are defined on the features object\n  sortFns, // now Fns are defined on the features object\n  // ...more features, row models, etc.\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features, // new required option\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n#### Shortcut: Use `stockFeatures` for Table V8-like Behavior\n\nIf you want all features without having to think about it (like Table V8), import `stockFeatures`:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useTable, stockFeatures } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features: stockFeatures, // All features included - just like Table V8 (though larger bundle now)\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n#### Available Features\n\n| Feature           | Import Name               |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------- |\n| Column Faceting   | `columnFacetingFeature`   |\n| Column Filtering  | `columnFilteringFeature`  |\n| Column Grouping   | `columnGroupingFeature`   |\n| Column Ordering   | `columnOrderingFeature`   |\n| Column Pinning    | `columnPinningFeature`    |\n| Column Resizing   | `columnResizingFeature`   |\n| Column Sizing     | `columnSizingFeature`     |\n| Column Visibility | `columnVisibilityFeature` |\n| Global Filtering  | `globalFilteringFeature`  |\n| Row Aggregation   | `rowAggregationFeature`   |\n| Row Expanding     | `rowExpandingFeature`     |\n| Row Pagination    | `rowPaginationFeature`    |\n| Row Pinning       | `rowPinningFeature`       |\n| Row Selection     | `rowSelectionFeature`     |\n| Row Sorting       | `rowSortingFeature`       |\n\n### Row Model Factories\n\nRow models are the functions that process your data (filtering, sorting, pagination, etc.). In Table V9, row model factories live on the `tableFeatures({})` call rather than a separate table option. The processing function registries (`filterFns`, `sortFns`, `aggregationFns`) are also registered on features. This enables better tree-shaking: you only bundle the row model code and filter/sort/aggregation functions you actually register.\n\nRow model slots are type-checked, so each row model must be specified after its associated feature in the same `tableFeatures` call.\n\n| Table V8 Option            | Table V9 `tableFeatures` Slot | Table V9 Factory Function     |\n| -------------------------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `getCoreRowModel()`        | (automatic)                   | Not needed, always included   |\n| `getFilteredRowModel()`    | `filteredRowModel`            | `createFilteredRowModel()`    |\n| `getSortedRowModel()`      | `sortedRowModel`              | `createSortedRowModel()`      |\n| `getPaginationRowModel()`  | `paginatedRowModel`           | `createPaginatedRowModel()`   |\n| `getExpandedRowModel()`    | `expandedRowModel`            | `createExpandedRowModel()`    |\n| `getGroupedRowModel()`     | `groupedRowModel`             | `createGroupedRowModel()`     |\n| `getFacetedRowModel()`     | `facetedRowModel`             | `createFacetedRowModel()`     |\n| `getFacetedMinMaxValues()` | `facetedMinMaxValues`         | `createFacetedMinMaxValues()` |\n| `getFacetedUniqueValues()` | `facetedUniqueValues`         | `createFacetedUniqueValues()` |\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  tableFeatures,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createGroupedRowModel,\n  filterFns, // Built-in filter functions\n  sortFns, // Built-in sort functions\n  aggregationFns, // Built-in aggregation functions\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowAggregationFeature,\n  columnGroupingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(),\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  filterFns,\n  sortFns,\n  aggregationFns,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n#### Prefer Individual Fn Imports Over Full Registries\n\nThe `filterFns`, `sortFns`, and `aggregationFns` registry exports are now deprecated in favor of importing individual `filterFn_*`, `sortFn_*`, and `aggregationFn_*` functions and registering only the ones you use (or passing functions directly in column definitions with no registration at all). The full registries still work, but spreading them puts every built-in function in your bundle. String names, including the default `'auto'`, only resolve functions you have registered.\n\n```tsx\n// Before: registers every built-in function\nimport { filterFns, sortFns } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  // ...other features and row models\n  filterFns,\n  sortFns,\n})\n\n// After: registers only the functions you use\nimport {\n  filterFn_includesString,\n  sortFn_alphanumeric,\n  sortFn_text,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  // ...other features and row models\n  filterFns: { includesString: filterFn_includesString },\n  sortFns: { alphanumeric: sortFn_alphanumeric, text: sortFn_text },\n})\n```\n\n### Instance Methods Must Be Called on Their Instance\n\nIn Table V9, methods on rows, cells, columns, headers, and similar table objects are shared on the object's prototype instead of being created as arrow functions on each object. This improves memory usage, but it means destructuring those methods loses the `this` context they need to operate on the instance.\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8 - worked because getValue closed over the row object\nconst { getValue } = row\nconst value = getValue('name')\n\n// Table V9 - call the method on the instance\nconst value = row.getValue('name')\n```\n\nThis applies to row, cell, column, header, and related instance APIs, but not to the table instance itself. Audit code that destructures methods from table objects or passes them around as bare callbacks. Prefer calling them through the original object, for example `row.getValue('name')`, `cell.getContext()`, `column.getCanSort()`, or `header.getContext()`.\n\nBecause these methods now live on the prototype, they also do not appear as own properties in `Object.keys(instance)`, object spread, or `JSON.stringify`. A shallow clone like `{ ...row }` copies row data but does not copy row methods. The methods are still callable normally because JavaScript looks them up through the prototype chain.\n\n---\n\n## State Management Changes\n\nTable V9's state system is built on [TanStack Store](https://tanstack.com/store) and exposes three read surfaces on the table instance:\n\n| Surface               | Type                                                                                                             | When to use                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `table.state`         | `TSelected` (full registered table state by default, or the shape returned from your custom `useTable` selector) | The most ergonomic read surface inside a component rendered by `useTable`.                                                                                                            |\n| `table.store`         | `ReadonlyStore<TableState>`                                                                                      | A flat, framework-agnostic store of the entire table state. Use `table.store.state` for one-off reads, or pair with `useSelector` / `table.Subscribe` for fine-grained subscriptions. |\n| `table.atoms.<slice>` | `ReadonlyAtom<TableState[slice]>`                                                                                | A per-slice readonly atom. Subscribe to a single slice (e.g. `table.atoms.sorting`) when you want the narrowest possible re-render surface.                                           |\n\nWritable counterparts (mostly internal):\n\n| Surface                   | Type                         | When to use                                                                                                                                    |\n| ------------------------- | ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `table.baseAtoms.<slice>` | `Atom<TableState[slice]>`    | The library's internal write target. You generally don't touch these directly; use `table.setSorting(...)`, `table.setPagination(...)`, etc.   |\n| `options.atoms`           | `Partial<{ [slice]: Atom }>` | Pass in your own writable atom for any slice to take ownership of that state externally. See [External Atoms](#external-atoms-advanced) below. |\n\n### Accessing State\n\nIn Table V8, you accessed state via `table.getState()`. In Table V9, state is accessed differently:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\nconst state = table.getState()\nconst { sorting, pagination } = table.getState()\n\n// Table V9 (RECOMMENDED) - via table.state (full selected state by default)\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\nconst { sorting, pagination } = table.state\n\n// Table V9 - via the store (full state)\nconst fullState = table.store.state\nconst { sorting, pagination } = table.store.state\n\n// Table V9 - via table.state with a custom selector\nconst selectedTable = useTable(options, (state) => ({\n  sorting: state.sorting,\n  pagination: state.pagination,\n}))\n// Now selectedTable.state only contains sorting and pagination\nconst { sorting, pagination } = selectedTable.state\n\n// Table V9 - via a single slice atom (framework-agnostic, ideal for fine-grained subscriptions)\nconst sorting = table.atoms.sorting.get()\n```\n\n#### onStateChange Table Option Removed\n\nThe `onStateChange` table option was removed in Table V9, although individual `on[State]Change` handlers are still available for specific state slices.\n\nIf you want to lift or listen to any state change, you can set up a subscription to the `table.store`:\n\n```tsx\nconst unsubscribe = table.store.subscribe((state) => {\n  console.log(state)\n})\n```\n\n### Optimized Rendering with `table.Subscribe`\n\nThe biggest state management improvement is `table.Subscribe`, which enables fine-grained reactivity:\n\n```tsx\nfunction MyTable() {\n  const table = useTable(\n    {\n      features,\n      columns,\n      data,\n    },\n    (state) => ({}), // default is state => state - this opts out of all default re-renders in favor of manual subscriptions down below\n  )\n\n  return (\n    <table.Subscribe\n      selector={(state) => ({\n        sorting: state.sorting,\n        pagination: state.pagination,\n      })}\n    >\n      {({ sorting, pagination }) => (\n        // This only re-renders when sorting or pagination changes\n        <div>\n          <table>{/* ... */}</table>\n          <div>Page {pagination.pageIndex + 1}</div>\n        </div>\n      )}\n    </table.Subscribe>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n### Default: Table V8-Style Full State Subscription\n\nThe default selector already gives Table V8-style behavior where the component re-renders on any registered table state change:\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n\n// table.state contains the full registered state\nconst { sorting, pagination, columnFilters } = table.state\n```\n\nPassing `(state) => state` is equivalent to the default and is no longer necessary. Pass a custom selector when you want `table.state` to contain only specific slices, or pass `() => null` and use `table.Subscribe` lower in the tree when the parent should not re-render for table state changes.\n\n### Controlled State\n\nThe Table V8-style `state` + `on[State]Change` controlled state patterns still work and remain convenient for simple integrations. For new Table V9 code, prefer owning state slices with external atoms (see [External Atoms](#external-atoms-advanced) below), which give you fine-grained subscriptions without mirroring state through React:\n\n```tsx\nconst [sorting, setSorting] = useState<SortingState>([])\nconst [pagination, setPagination] = useState<PaginationState>({\n  pageIndex: 0,\n  pageSize: 10,\n})\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n  state: {\n    sorting,\n    pagination,\n  },\n  onSortingChange: setSorting,\n  onPaginationChange: setPagination,\n})\n```\n\n### Per-Slice Atom Subscriptions\n\nBecause each state slice is backed by its own atom, you can subscribe a component to a single slice without re-rendering on any other state change. Use `useSelector` from `@tanstack/react-store` with `table.atoms.<slice>`:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useSelector } from '@tanstack/react-store'\n\nfunction PaginationFooter({ table }) {\n  // Re-renders only when pagination changes. Sorting, filtering, selection, etc. are all ignored.\n  const pagination = useSelector(table.atoms.pagination)\n\n  return <div>Page {pagination.pageIndex + 1}</div>\n}\n```\n\nThis is the narrowest subscription surface available. Compared to `table.Subscribe`, which selects from the full `table.store.state`, reading a per-slice atom skips even constructing the full state snapshot on change.\n\n> **When to reach for** `table.atoms` **vs.** `table.Subscribe`**:** Both give you fine-grained re-renders. `table.Subscribe` is nicer when you want to project multiple slices into a single rendered block. `table.atoms.<slice>` is nicer when a component only cares about one slice, or when you're passing a subscription source to non-table code.\n\n### External Atoms (Advanced)\n\nFor advanced patterns (sharing a slice across tables, integrating with atom-based libraries, or wiring a slice up to persistence), Table V9 lets you **own individual state slices yourself** by passing writable atoms via the new `atoms` option. See the [Basic External Atoms example](../examples/basic-external-atoms).\n\n```tsx\nimport { useCreateAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/react-store'\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n  sortFns,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\nimport type { PaginationState, SortingState } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature, rowPaginationFeature })\n\nfunction MyTable({ data, columns }) {\n  // Create stable external atoms for the slices you want to own.\n  const sortingAtom = useCreateAtom<SortingState>([])\n  const paginationAtom = useCreateAtom<PaginationState>({\n    pageIndex: 0,\n    pageSize: 10,\n  })\n\n  // Subscribe to each atom independently for fine-grained reactivity.\n  const sorting = useSelector(sortingAtom)\n  const pagination = useSelector(paginationAtom)\n\n  const table = useTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    data,\n    // Per-slice external atoms. The library writes directly to these,\n    // bypassing the internal baseAtoms for those slices.\n    atoms: {\n      sorting: sortingAtom,\n      pagination: paginationAtom,\n    },\n  })\n\n  // Table writes like table.setPageIndex(2) go straight to `paginationAtom`.\n  // Any other subscriber of `paginationAtom` will see the update too.\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\n#### How External Atoms Interact with `state` and `on*Change`\n\nWhen you register an external atom for a slice:\n\n- **Reads**: The derived `table.atoms[slice]` and `table.store.state[slice]` both read from your external atom.\n- **Writes**: Library writes (e.g. `table.setSorting(...)`, `column.toggleSorting()`) go directly to your external atom's `set()`. You do **not** need a corresponding `onSortingChange` handler; owning the atom is the subscription.\n- **Precedence**: If you pass both `options.atoms[key]` and `options.state[key]`, the atom wins. If you pass neither, Table V9 falls back to its internal `baseAtoms[key]` (Table V8-style self-managed state).\n- **Reset**: `table.reset()` does **not** clear external atoms. You own them, so you decide when to reset. Call `myAtom.set(defaultValue)` yourself if needed.\n\n#### When to Choose External Atoms vs. Controlled State\n\n| Pattern                                                 | Use when                                                                                                                                                      |\n| ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Internal state (no `state`, no `atoms`)                 | Simplest path; the table manages everything.                                                                                                                  |\n| `state` + `on*Change` (Table V8-style controlled state) | You want your framework's idiomatic state (React `useState`, signals, etc.) to own the slice.                                                                 |\n| `atoms` option                                          | You want atom-based ergonomics (cross-component subscriptions, `useSelector`, `useAtom`) without the overhead of mirroring between React state and the table. |\n\n---\n\n## Feature-by-Feature Breaking Changes\n\n### Sorting\n\nSorting-related APIs have been renamed for consistency:\n\n| Table V8                          | Table V9                 |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------------------ |\n| `sortingFn` (column def option)   | `sortFn`                 |\n| `column.getSortingFn()`           | `column.getSortFn()`     |\n| `column.getAutoSortingFn()`       | `column.getAutoSortFn()` |\n| `SortingFn` type                  | `SortFn` type            |\n| `SortingFns` interface            | `SortFns` interface      |\n| `sortingFns` (built-in functions) | `sortFns`                |\n\nUpdate your column definitions:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n    sortingFn: 'alphanumeric', // or custom function\n  },\n]\n\n// Table V9\nconst columns = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'name',\n    sortFn: 'alphanumeric', // or custom function\n  },\n]\n```\n\n### Column Pinning\n\nV9 changes column pinning to use logical `start`/`end` terminology instead of the physical `left`/`right` terminology used in V8. In LTR languages/layouts, `start` usually corresponds to left and `end` to right; in RTL languages/layouts, `start` usually corresponds to right and `end` to left. There are no deprecated aliases.\n\n| V8                                   | V9                                   |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------ |\n| `columnPinning.left`                 | `columnPinning.start`                |\n| `columnPinning.right`                | `columnPinning.end`                  |\n| `column.pin('left')`                 | `column.pin('start')`                |\n| `column.pin('right')`                | `column.pin('end')`                  |\n| `column.getIsPinned() === 'left'`    | `column.getIsPinned() === 'start'`   |\n| `column.getIsPinned() === 'right'`   | `column.getIsPinned() === 'end'`     |\n| `row.getLeftVisibleCells()`          | `row.getStartVisibleCells()`         |\n| `row.getRightVisibleCells()`         | `row.getEndVisibleCells()`           |\n| `table.getLeftHeaderGroups()`        | `table.getStartHeaderGroups()`       |\n| `table.getRightHeaderGroups()`       | `table.getEndHeaderGroups()`         |\n| `table.getLeftLeafColumns()`         | `table.getStartLeafColumns()`        |\n| `table.getRightLeafColumns()`        | `table.getEndLeafColumns()`          |\n| `table.getLeftVisibleLeafColumns()`  | `table.getStartVisibleLeafColumns()` |\n| `table.getRightVisibleLeafColumns()` | `table.getEndVisibleLeafColumns()`   |\n| `table.getLeftTotalSize()`           | `table.getStartTotalSize()`          |\n| `table.getRightTotalSize()`          | `table.getEndTotalSize()`            |\n| `column.getStart('left')`            | `column.getStart('start')`           |\n| `column.getAfter('right')`           | `column.getAfter('end')`             |\n| `column.getIndex('left')`            | `column.getIndex('start')`           |\n| `column.getIndex('right')`           | `column.getIndex('end')`             |\n\nThis rename is about logical table regions, not automatic DOM direction handling. For sticky column pinning, prefer CSS logical properties like `insetInlineStart` and `insetInlineEnd`. The `columnResizeDirection` table option is unchanged.\n\nThe `enablePinning` option has also been split into separate options:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\nenablePinning: true\n\n// Table V9\nenableColumnPinning: true\nenableRowPinning: true\n```\n\n### Column Sizing vs. Column Resizing Split\n\nIn Table V8, column sizing and resizing were combined in a single feature. In Table V9, they've been split into separate features for better tree-shaking.\n\n| Table V8                          | Table V9                                        |\n| --------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `ColumnSizing` (combined feature) | `columnSizingFeature` + `columnResizingFeature` |\n| `columnSizingInfo` state          | `columnResizing` state                          |\n| `setColumnSizingInfo()`           | `setColumnResizing()`                           |\n| `onColumnSizingInfoChange` option | `onColumnResizingChange` option                 |\n\nIf you only need column sizing (fixed widths) without interactive resizing, you can import just `columnSizingFeature`. If you need drag-to-resize functionality, import both:\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnSizingFeature,\n  columnResizingFeature, // Only if you need interactive resizing\n})\n```\n\n### Grouping and Aggregation\n\nAggregation is now its own feature, independent from column grouping. `stockFeatures` still includes both, so tables using it need no feature-registration change. If you declare features explicitly, add `rowAggregationFeature` whenever columns use `aggregationFn`, `aggregatedCell`, `getAggregationValue`, or `cell.getIsAggregated`. Add `columnGroupingFeature` and `groupedRowModel` only when you also group rows.\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowAggregationFeature,\n  columnGroupingFeature, // only for grouped rows\n  groupedRowModel: createGroupedRowModel(),\n  aggregationFns: { sum: aggregationFn_sum },\n})\n```\n\nCustom aggregation callables have changed to context-based definitions:\n\n```ts\n// Table V8/earlier V9 betas\nconst total = (columnId, leafRows, childRows) =>\n  leafRows.reduce((sum, row) => sum + row.getValue(columnId), 0)\n\n// Current V9\nconst total = constructAggregationFn({\n  aggregate: ({ rows, getValue }) =>\n    rows.reduce((sum, row) => sum + Number(getValue(row)), 0),\n})\n```\n\nThe old per-function choice between `childRows` and `leafRows` is replaced by a single depth-selected `context.rows`, controlled by the `maxAggregationDepth` column option. The default (`0`) preserves V8's direct-child grouped aggregation; use `Infinity` to aggregate terminal leaf rows.\n\n`column.getAggregationValue()` now takes a single options object instead of positional arguments:\n\n```ts\n// Table V8/earlier V9 betas\ncolumn.getAggregationValue(rows, maxDepth)\n\n// Current V9\ncolumn.getAggregationValue({ rows, maxDepth })\n```\n\n`column.getAggregationFn()` is now `column.getAggregationFns()` because a column can run multiple definitions, and the old callable `AggregationFn`/`CreatedAggregationFn` types are replaced by `AggregationFnDef`.\n\nSee the [Grouping Guide](./grouping) and the [Aggregation Guide](./aggregation) for full documentation of the new capabilities.\n\n### Row Selection\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Minor breaking change:** `row.getToggleSelectedHandler()` now enables inclusive Shift range selection by default when `rowSelectionFeature` is enabled. Existing checkboxes or rows wired through this handler establish an anchor on an ordinary interaction and select or deselect the current display-order range on a Shift interaction. Direct `row.toggleSelected()` calls are unchanged.\n>\n> Set `enableRowRangeSelection: false` to preserve the previous non-range handler behavior. The handler must receive an event that exposes Shift directly or through `nativeEvent`; see [Shift Range Selection](./row-selection.md#shift-range-selection).\n\nThe \"some rows selected\" checks were simplified to mean \"at least one row is selected\":\n\n| API                                 | Table V8                                            | Table V9                                      |\n| ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `table.getIsSomeRowsSelected()`     | `true` when some but not all rows are selected      | `true` when at least one row is selected      |\n| `table.getIsSomePageRowsSelected()` | `true` when some but not all page rows are selected | `true` when at least one page row is selected |\n\nIn Table V8 these returned `false` once every row was selected; in Table V9 they stay `true`. If you use them to drive an indeterminate \"select all\" checkbox, gate the indeterminate state on the matching all-selected check so it clears at full selection:\n\n`getIsSomeRowsSelected() && !getIsAllRowsSelected()`\n\n### Row and Internal API Changes\n\nSome row APIs have changed from private to public:\n\n| Table V8                                 | Table V9                               |\n| ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| `row._getAllCellsByColumnId()` (private) | `row.getAllCellsByColumnId()` (public) |\n\nAll other internal APIs prefixed with `_` have been removed. If you were using any of these, use their public equivalents:\n\n- Removed: `table._getPinnedRows()`\n- Removed: `table._getFacetedRowModel()`\n- Removed: `table._getFacetedMinMaxValues()`\n- Removed: `table._getFacetedUniqueValues()`\n\n---\n\n## Column Helper Changes\n\nThe `createColumnHelper` function now requires a `TFeatures` type parameter in addition to `TData`:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\nimport { createColumnHelper } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<Person>()\n\n// Table V9\nimport {\n  createColumnHelper,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature })\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n```\n\n### New `columns()` Helper Method\n\nTable V9 adds a `columns()` helper for better type inference when wrapping column arrays. In Table V8, `TValue` wasn't always type-safe, especially with group columns, where nested column types could be lost or widened. The `columns()` helper uses variadic tuple types to preserve each column's individual `TValue` type, so `info.getValue()` and cell renderers stay correctly typed throughout nested structures:\n\n```tsx\nconst columnHelper = createColumnHelper<typeof features, Person>()\n\n// Wrap your columns array for better type inference\nconst columns = columnHelper.columns([\n  columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  }),\n  columnHelper.accessor('lastName', {\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => <span>Last Name</span>,\n    cell: (info) => <i>{info.getValue()}</i>,\n  }),\n  columnHelper.display({\n    id: 'actions',\n    header: 'Actions',\n    cell: (info) => <button>Edit</button>,\n  }),\n])\n```\n\n---\n\n## Rendering Changes\n\n### `flexRender` Function\n\nThe `flexRender` function still exists and works the same way:\n\n```tsx\nimport { flexRender } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\n// Still works in Table V9\nflexRender(cell.column.columnDef.cell, cell.getContext())\nflexRender(header.column.columnDef.header, header.getContext())\n```\n\n### New `FlexRender` and `<table.FlexRender />` Component\n\nTable V9 adds a cleaner component-based approach attached to the table instance:\n\n```tsx\nconst table = useTable({ /* ... */ })\n\n// Instead of:\n{flexRender(header.column.columnDef.header, header.getContext())}\n\n// You can use:\n<table.FlexRender header={header} />\n<table.FlexRender cell={cell} />\n<table.FlexRender footer={footer} />\n// or\nimport { FlexRender } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n<FlexRender header={header} />\n<FlexRender cell={cell} />\n<FlexRender footer={footer} />\n```\n\nThis should be way more convenient than the old `flexRender` function!\n\n---\n\n## The `tableOptions()` Utility\n\nThe `tableOptions()` helper provides type-safe composition of table options. It's useful for creating reusable partial configurations that can be spread into your table setup.\n\n### Basic Usage\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  tableOptions,\n  tableFeatures,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ rowSortingFeature })\n\n// Create a reusable options object with features pre-configured\nconst baseOptions = tableOptions({\n  features,\n  debugTable: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development',\n})\n\n// Use in your table; columns, data, and other options can be added\nconst table = useTable({\n  ...baseOptions,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n### Composing Partial Options\n\n`tableOptions()` lets you omit required fields (like `data`, `columns`, or `features`) when creating partial configurations:\n\n```tsx\n// Row model factories and fns registries are registered on the features object\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n  filterFns,\n})\n\n// Partial options without data or columns\nconst featureOptions = tableOptions({\n  features,\n  getRowId: (row) => row.id,\n  manualPagination: true,\n  manualSorting: true,\n  manualFiltering: true,\n})\n\n// Another partial without features (inherits from spread)\nconst paginationDefaults = tableOptions({\n  initialState: {\n    pagination: { pageIndex: 0, pageSize: 25 },\n  },\n})\n\n// Combine them\nconst table = useTable({\n  ...featureOptions,\n  ...paginationDefaults,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n### Using with `createTableHook`\n\n`tableOptions()` pairs well with `createTableHook` for building composable table factories:\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst sharedOptions = tableOptions({ features })\n\nconst { useAppTable } = createTableHook(sharedOptions)\n```\n\n---\n\n## `createTableHook`: Composable Table Patterns\n\n**This is an advanced, optional feature.** You don't need to use `createTableHook`; `useTable` is sufficient for most use cases. If you're familiar with [TanStack Form](https://tanstack.com/form)'s `createFormHook`, `createTableHook` works almost the same way: it creates a custom hook with pre-bound configuration that you can reuse across many tables.\n\nFor applications with multiple tables sharing the same configuration, `createTableHook` lets you define features (including row model factories), and reusable components once:\n\n```tsx\n// hooks/table.ts\nimport {\n  createTableHook,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  createFilteredRowModel,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createPaginatedRowModel,\n  filterFns,\n  sortFns,\n} from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\n// Import your reusable components\nimport { PaginationControls, SortIndicator, TextCell } from './components'\n\n// Features and row model factories defined once\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  rowPaginationFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  paginatedRowModel: createPaginatedRowModel(),\n  filterFns,\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nexport const {\n  useAppTable,\n  createAppColumnHelper,\n  useTableContext,\n  useCellContext,\n  useHeaderContext,\n} = createTableHook({\n  features,\n\n  // Default table options\n  debugTable: process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development',\n\n  // Register reusable components\n  tableComponents: { PaginationControls },\n  cellComponents: { TextCell },\n  headerComponents: { SortIndicator },\n})\n```\n\n### Using `useAppTable`\n\n```tsx\n// features/users.tsx\nimport { useAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } from './hooks/table'\n\nconst columnHelper = createAppColumnHelper<Person>()\n\nconst columns = columnHelper.columns([\n  columnHelper.accessor('firstName', {\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: ({ cell }) => <cell.TextCell />, // Pre-bound component!\n  }),\n])\n\nfunction UsersTable({ data }: { data: Person[] }) {\n  const table = useAppTable({\n    columns,\n    data,\n    // features (including row model factories) already configured!\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <table.AppTable>\n      <table>\n        <thead>\n          {table.getHeaderGroups().map((headerGroup) => (\n            <tr key={headerGroup.id}>\n              {headerGroup.headers.map((h) => (\n                <table.AppHeader header={h} key={h.id}>\n                  {(header) => (\n                    <th>\n                      <header.FlexRender />\n                      <header.SortIndicator />\n                    </th>\n                  )}\n                </table.AppHeader>\n              ))}\n            </tr>\n          ))}\n        </thead>\n        <tbody>\n          {table.getRowModel().rows.map((row) => (\n            <tr key={row.id}>\n              {row.getAllCells().map((c) => (\n                <table.AppCell cell={c} key={c.id}>\n                  {(cell) => (\n                    <td>\n                      <cell.FlexRender />\n                    </td>\n                  )}\n                </table.AppCell>\n              ))}\n            </tr>\n          ))}\n        </tbody>\n      </table>\n      <table.PaginationControls />\n    </table.AppTable>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n### Context Hooks for Components\n\nComponents registered via `createTableHook` can access their context:\n\n```tsx\n// components/SortIndicator.tsx\nimport { useHeaderContext } from './hooks/table'\n\nexport function SortIndicator() {\n  const header = useHeaderContext()\n  const sorted = header.column.getIsSorted()\n\n  if (!sorted) return null\n  return sorted === 'asc' ? ' 🔼' : ' 🔽'\n}\n\n// components/TextCell.tsx\nimport { useCellContext } from './hooks/table'\n\nexport function TextCell() {\n  const cell = useCellContext()\n  return <span>{cell.getValue() as string}</span>\n}\n\n// components/PaginationControls.tsx\nimport { useTableContext } from './hooks/table'\n\nexport function PaginationControls() {\n  const table = useTableContext()\n\n  return (\n    <table.Subscribe selector={(s) => s.pagination}>\n      {(pagination) => (\n        <div>\n          <button onClick={() => table.previousPage()}>Previous</button>\n          <span>Page {pagination.pageIndex + 1}</span>\n          <button onClick={() => table.nextPage()}>Next</button>\n        </div>\n      )}\n    </table.Subscribe>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## TypeScript Changes Summary\n\n### Type Generics\n\nMost types now require a `TFeatures` parameter:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\ntype Column<TData>\ntype ColumnDef<TData>\ntype Table<TData>\ntype Row<TData>\ntype Cell<TData, TValue>\n\n// Table V9\ntype Column<TFeatures, TData, TValue>\ntype ColumnDef<TFeatures, TData, TValue>\ntype Table<TFeatures, TData>\ntype Row<TFeatures, TData>\ntype Cell<TFeatures, TData, TValue>\n```\n\n### Using `typeof features`\n\nThe easiest way to get the `TFeatures` type is with `typeof`:\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n})\n\n// Use typeof to get the type\ntype MyFeatures = typeof features\n\nconst columns: ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>[] = [...]\n\nfunction Filter({ column }: { column: Column<typeof features, Person, unknown> }) {\n  // ...\n}\n```\n\n### Using `StockFeatures`\n\nIf using `stockFeatures` with `useTable`, use the `StockFeatures` type:\n\n```tsx\nimport type { StockFeatures, ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/react-table'\n\nconst columns: ColumnDef<StockFeatures, Person>[] = [...]\n```\n\n### `TableMeta`/`ColumnMeta` Typing Changes\n\nNo more declaration merging required! (Although it still works if you want to keep using it)\n\nGlobal declaration merging to extend `TableMeta` or `ColumnMeta` works exactly like it did in Table V8. The only change you need to make is updating the generics shape: both interfaces now take `TFeatures` as the first type parameter.\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-table' {\n  interface ColumnMeta<TData, TValue> {\n    customProperty: string\n  }\n}\n\n// Table V9 - TFeatures is now the first parameter\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-table' {\n  interface ColumnMeta<TFeatures, TData, TValue> {\n    customProperty: string\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThat's all that's required if you want to keep declaring meta types globally.\n\nOptionally, Table V9 also adds a new way to declare meta types **per-table** without declaration merging. You can use type-only `tableMeta`/`columnMeta` slots on the `features` option, which only affect tables created with that `features` object:\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  columnMeta: metaHelper<{ customProperty: string }>(),\n})\n```\n\nSee the new [Table and Column Meta Guide](../../../guide/table-and-column-meta) for full details on both approaches.\n\n### `FilterFns`/`SortFns`/`AggregationFns`/`FilterMeta` Augmentation Replaced by Registry Slots\n\nIn Table V8, making a custom function usable as a string reference (like `filterFn: 'fuzzy'`) required `declare module` augmentation of the `FilterFns` interface, and typing filter meta required augmenting `FilterMeta`. In Table V9, registering the function in the matching registry slot does both jobs with no global augmentation:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8\ndeclare module '@tanstack/react-table' {\n  interface FilterFns {\n    fuzzy: FilterFn<unknown>\n  }\n  interface FilterMeta {\n    itemRank: RankingInfo\n  }\n}\n\n// Table V9 - register in the slot; the key becomes a valid string value\ninterface FuzzyFilterMeta {\n  itemRank?: RankingInfo\n}\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  columnFilteringFeature,\n  filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel(),\n  filterFns: { fuzzy: fuzzyFilter },\n  filterMeta: metaHelper<FuzzyFilterMeta>(),\n})\n\n// 'fuzzy' now typechecks in column defs for tables using these features\ncolumnHelper.accessor('name', { filterFn: 'fuzzy' })\n```\n\nThe same pattern applies to `sortFns` (for `sortFn` string values) and `aggregationFns` (for `aggregationFn` string values). See the [Fuzzy Filtering Guide](./fuzzy-filtering.md) for a complete example.\n\n### `RowData` Type Restriction\n\nThe `RowData` type is now more restrictive:\n\n```tsx\n// Table V8 - very permissive\ntype RowData = unknown\n\n// Table V9 - must be a record or array\ntype RowData = Record<string, any> | Array<any>\n```\n\nThis change improves type safety. If you were passing unusual data types, ensure your data conforms to `Record<string, any>` or `Array<any>`.\n\n---\n\n## Migration Checklist\n\n- [ ] Update import: `useReactTable` → `useTable`\n- [ ] Define `features` using `tableFeatures()` (or use `stockFeatures`)\n- [ ] Migrate `get*RowModel()` options to `tableFeatures` slots (e.g. `filteredRowModel: createFilteredRowModel()`)\n- [ ] Register `filterFns` / `sortFns` / `aggregationFns` registries as slots on `tableFeatures` (row model factories no longer take arguments)\n- [ ] Replace destructured row/cell/column/header methods with calls on the instance (for example, `row.getValue('name')`)\n- [ ] Rename `sortingFn` → `sortFn` in column definitions\n- [ ] Update column pinning to `start`/`end` terminology (`columnPinning.start`, `column.pin('end')`, `getStart*`/`getEnd*` APIs)\n- [ ] Replace `enablePinning` with `enableColumnPinning`/`enableRowPinning` if used\n- [ ] Rename `columnSizingInfo` state → `columnResizing` (and related options)\n- [ ] Convert custom aggregation callables to `constructAggregationFn({ aggregate, merge? })` definitions\n- [ ] Update state access: `table.getState()` → `table.store.state` or `table.state`\n- [ ] Update TypeScript types to include `TFeatures` generic\n- [ ] Update `createColumnHelper<TData>()` → `createColumnHelper<TFeatures, TData>()`\n- [ ] If you use `TableMeta`/`ColumnMeta` declaration merging, add the `TFeatures` generic to your augmentations (optionally, switch to the per-table `tableMeta`/`columnMeta` feature slots)\n- [ ] Replace `declare module` augmentation of `FilterFns`/`SortFns`/`AggregationFns` with registry-slot registration, and `FilterMeta` augmentation with the `filterMeta` slot\n- [ ] (Optional) Add `table.Subscribe` for render optimizations\n- [ ] (Optional) Subscribe to individual slices via `table.atoms.<slice>` + `useSelector` for the narrowest re-renders\n- [ ] (Optional) Pass writable atoms via the new `atoms` option to own specific state slices externally\n- [ ] (Optional) Use `tableOptions()` for composable configurations\n- [ ] (Optional) Migrate to `createTableHook` for reusable table patterns\n\n---\n\n## Examples\n\nCheck out these examples to see Table V9 patterns in action:\n\n- [Basic useTable](../examples/basic-use-table) - Simple table with the new `useTable` hook\n- [Basic useLegacyTable](../examples/basic-use-legacy-table) - Migration example using `useLegacyTable`\n- [Basic useAppTable](../examples/basic-use-app-table) - Using `createTableHook`\n- [Basic External State](../examples/basic-external-state) - Classic `state` + `on*Change` controlled state\n- [Basic External Atoms](../examples/basic-external-atoms) - Owning state slices with `useCreateAtom` + the `atoms` option\n- [Filters](../examples/filters) - Filtering with the new API\n- [Sorting](../examples/sorting) - Sorting with the new API\n- [Composable Tables](../examples/composable-tables) - Advanced `createTableHook` patterns\n- [Kitchen Sink](../examples/kitchen-sink) - An example combining all features\n"}
